WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.895 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.895 --> 00:00:02.395 align:middle line:90%


00:00:02.395 --> 00:00:03.811 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.811 --> 00:00:04.311 align:middle line:90%


00:00:04.311 --> 00:00:05.748 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.748 --> 00:00:11.030 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:15.082 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
All right, so one

00:00:15.082 --> 00:00:16.790 align:middle line:84%
of the things we've
learned in this class

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.590 align:middle line:90%
is that language is complicated.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.870 align:middle line:84%
At least, maybe you
already knew that,

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.060 align:middle line:84%
but we've spent a
semester looking

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.308 align:middle line:84%
at various ways in which
language is more complicated

00:00:26.308 --> 00:00:28.850 align:middle line:84%
than you might have-- than you
might have realized before you

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:30.475 align:middle line:84%
came in here and in
ways that maybe you

00:00:30.475 --> 00:00:33.032 align:middle line:90%
hadn't thought about much.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:34.490 align:middle line:84%
These are a few of
the things we've

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.790 align:middle line:84%
talked about, various special
properties of language,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:41.300 align:middle line:84%
fancy things that language does
that we've spent the semester

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.290 align:middle line:90%
puzzling over.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:47.940 align:middle line:84%
And if language has all of
these complicated properties,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.180 align:middle line:84%
the recurring question, which
I've raised a couple of times,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.270 align:middle line:90%
is how do we acquire this?

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.850 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk seriously
about language acquisition

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.200 align:middle line:90%
sometime next week.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.510 align:middle line:84%
But a provisional answer to
this that people have offered

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.320 align:middle line:84%
and taken seriously is that
at least some of this stuff

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.510 align:middle line:90%
is innate, in the sense that--

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.220 align:middle line:84%
I think I've said
it this way before--

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.470 align:middle line:84%
part of being a
human being is having

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.200 align:middle line:84%
the kind of mind that constructs
language in certain ways,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.020 align:middle line:90%
but not others.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.640 align:middle line:84%
So if we ask questions
like, why do languages

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.110 align:middle line:90%
have binary branching?

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.120 align:middle line:90%
Why not ternary branching?

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.800 align:middle line:84%
The answer is, well,
we're not equipped

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.120 align:middle line:90%
with ternary branching.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.850 align:middle line:84%
The human brain does it this
way and not some other way.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.150 align:middle line:84%
And that leads to lots of
other interesting questions,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:33.840 align:middle line:90%
like why?

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.910 align:middle line:84%
What is it about human minds
that are set up this way?

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.110 align:middle line:84%
Can we get that to follow
from anything else, any deeper

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.010 align:middle line:90%
conditions?

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.910 align:middle line:84%
But that's a claim
that's out there,

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.890 align:middle line:84%
which we have referred
to a couple of times.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.407 align:middle line:84%
We don't start
with a blank slate.

00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:53.490 align:middle line:84%
We start with a rich body
of linguistic knowledge,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.323 align:middle line:84%
and learning our actual
language is a matter

00:01:55.323 --> 00:01:57.090 align:middle line:90%
of filling in some details.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.880 align:middle line:90%


00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:04.410 align:middle line:84%
But it's very clear that
not everything is innate.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.810 align:middle line:90%
For example, here's a cartoon.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.000 align:middle line:90%
It has a cat in it.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.990 align:middle line:84%
And the fact that animal
is pronounced "cat"

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.850 align:middle line:90%
is an accident.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.610 align:middle line:84%
It could have been
something else.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.520 align:middle line:84%
So Saussure famously pointed
this out and called it

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.310 align:middle line:84%
the arbitrariness
of the sign, that,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.780 align:middle line:84%
for the most part, what
word the language has

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.302 align:middle line:84%
for a given thing has to do
with the phonetic inventory

00:02:31.302 --> 00:02:33.510 align:middle line:84%
of the language and other
properties of the language,

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.062 align:middle line:84%
but the English word
"cat" refers to cats,

00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:38.770 align:middle line:84%
but that's kind of a
historical accident.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.640 align:middle line:84%
It could have referred to
dogs or mice or anything else.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.620 align:middle line:90%
Just happens to refer to cats.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:46.250 align:middle line:90%


00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.100 align:middle line:84%
And the fact is that kids, if
you're ever around small kids,

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:55.250 align:middle line:84%
you'll hear them trying out
theories about what words mean

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.740 align:middle line:90%
and being wrong.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.170 align:middle line:84%
I was once around a small
child who used the word

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.780 align:middle line:90%
"moon" for every light.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.530 align:middle line:84%
So the hall light was a moon,
and the moon was a moon,

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:07.820 align:middle line:90%
and the sun was a moon.

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.910 align:middle line:90%
Everything was a moon.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:16.680 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes these mistakes
survive to adulthood.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.508 align:middle line:90%
So take the word "livid."

00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:21.050 align:middle line:84%
What do you think
of the word "livid"

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.930 align:middle line:84%
as meaning, if you're a
native speaker of English

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.010 align:middle line:90%
or even if you're not?

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:27.150 align:middle line:90%
Very angry.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.400 align:middle line:90%
That's what it often means.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.910 align:middle line:90%
It's originally a color term.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:34.800 align:middle line:90%
It meant white.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.470 align:middle line:84%
Because there was an idea that
when people got really angry,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.730 align:middle line:90%
their faces turned white.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.400 align:middle line:90%
They were white with rage.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.620 align:middle line:84%
You sometimes see
the expression,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.490 align:middle line:90%
his face went livid.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.073 align:middle line:84%
When I was a kid, I
thought that meant

00:03:46.073 --> 00:03:48.210 align:middle line:84%
his face was red because
I think of people

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.940 align:middle line:90%
who are angry as being red.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.955 align:middle line:84%
But originally, that's
what the word meant.

00:03:52.955 --> 00:03:53.580 align:middle line:90%
It meant white.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.230 align:middle line:84%
And then yes, I think for most
of us, now it means angry.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.532 align:middle line:84%
Or I used to believe in
the verb "misle" until--

00:04:01.532 --> 00:04:03.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm embarrassed to say when I
stopped believing in the word

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:04.620 align:middle line:90%
"misle."

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.438 align:middle line:84%
I was an adult. Did anybody
else have the verb "misle"?

00:04:08.438 --> 00:04:10.230 align:middle line:84%
Is there anybody who
right now is thinking,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:11.355 align:middle line:90%
what are you talking about?

00:04:11.355 --> 00:04:13.200 align:middle line:84%
Of course, there's
a verb "misle."

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.510 align:middle line:84%
I believed in the verb
"misle" because I had read it

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:18.120 align:middle line:90%
in expressions like this.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:23.570 align:middle line:84%
I had been "misle-d"
was what I believed.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.680 align:middle line:90%
It wasn't-- I think--

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.100 align:middle line:84%
I don't believe I actually
used the word in conversation

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.270 align:middle line:90%
and had somebody correct me.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.390 align:middle line:84%
I think this was, like, I
was in my 20s or something

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:37.570 align:middle line:84%
when I sat down and was
like, wait a minute.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:38.850 align:middle line:90%
So stuff like this happens.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:41.730 align:middle line:90%


00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.850 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes these mistakes
catch on, and people--

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.330 align:middle line:84%
and sort of survive
into adulthood.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.060 align:middle line:84%
As I say, I was in
my 20s, and I still

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.280 align:middle line:84%
believed that there
was a verb "misle."

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.750 align:middle line:84%
If I were a king
or something, then

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.225 align:middle line:84%
if my subjects
tried to convince me

00:04:56.225 --> 00:04:57.600 align:middle line:84%
that there wasn't
a verb "misle,"

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.933 align:middle line:90%
I would just have them beheaded.

00:04:58.933 --> 00:05:00.360 align:middle line:84%
And before we knew
it, there would

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.190 align:middle line:90%
be a verb "misle" in English.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:09.030 align:middle line:84%
Or if I were somebody else
who was hugely, hugely

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:13.230 align:middle line:84%
influential, like a linguistics
professor or something.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.180 align:middle line:90%
Wait a minute.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.250 align:middle line:84%
There are also various
forms of semantic drift

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.050 align:middle line:84%
that languages
undergo, where words

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.950 align:middle line:90%
change what they refer to.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:24.520 align:middle line:84%
"Livid" is maybe
an example of that.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.550 align:middle line:84%
It used to mean white,
and now it means angry.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.760 align:middle line:90%
Here's another example.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.700 align:middle line:84%
The modern English word "bead"
originally meant a prayer.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.100 align:middle line:84%
So there's still-- there's
a German verb "beten."

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.210 align:middle line:90%
That's the verb "to pray."

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.970 align:middle line:84%
It's related to the
English word "bead"--

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.470 align:middle line:84%
in Old English, the ancestor of
the word "bead" meant a prayer.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:48.660 align:middle line:84%
The switch from prayer to bead,
you know, small, round object,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.390 align:middle line:84%
came via rosaries,
which are devices

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:55.090 align:middle line:84%
for counting prayers in
certain religious traditions,

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.558 align:middle line:84%
including Catholicism
and others.

00:05:57.558 --> 00:05:59.100 align:middle line:84%
So if you're in a
religious tradition

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.260 align:middle line:84%
where it's important to say
a certain number of prayers,

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.302 align:middle line:84%
you have this little device
that has beads on it.

00:06:03.302 --> 00:06:06.505 align:middle line:84%
And you're counting the
prayers using this device.

00:06:06.505 --> 00:06:08.130 align:middle line:84%
So if you're asked,
what are you doing?

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:09.270 align:middle line:90%
Well, you're counting prayers.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:10.200 align:middle line:84%
But there's another
sense in which

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.930 align:middle line:84%
you're counting, well, beads,
like small, round objects

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:13.990 align:middle line:90%
on a wire.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:19.940 align:middle line:84%
And this is thought to be how
the word shifted its meaning.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:25.130 align:middle line:84%
Or there are other things like
this, words widen or narrow

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:26.120 align:middle line:90%
their meanings.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.150 align:middle line:84%
In Old English,
"steorfan" meant "to die."

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.310 align:middle line:84%
It's cognate with
German "sterben."

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:34.100 align:middle line:84%
It's the ancestor of
modern English "starve,"

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.450 align:middle line:90%
which went through a stage of--

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:39.800 align:middle line:90%
sort of originally meant "die."

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.758 align:middle line:84%
It went through a stage
of meaning specifically

00:06:41.758 --> 00:06:43.100 align:middle line:90%
"die from hunger."

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.250 align:middle line:84%
And today, I think of it
as basically just meaning,

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.110 align:middle line:90%
be very hungry.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.060 align:middle line:84%
So you can still
say "He starved,"

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:53.210 align:middle line:84%
and have that mean
he died, I guess.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.600 align:middle line:84%
But I'm a lot more likely
to use it in an expression

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:57.450 align:middle line:90%
like "I'm starving.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.780 align:middle line:90%
Let's have lunch."

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.430 align:middle line:84%
Maybe when I say
that, I literally

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.410 align:middle line:84%
mean I'm dying of
hunger, or figuratively

00:07:03.410 --> 00:07:06.440 align:middle line:90%
I mean I'm dying of hunger.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.000 align:middle line:84%
Or there's a French word
"negre" which means a Black man.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.700 align:middle line:84%
It has a descendant in
modern Haitian Creole,

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.700 align:middle line:90%
"neg," which just means man.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.550 align:middle line:84%
So the meaning of
that has broadened

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.990 align:middle line:84%
for reasons that are not
all that hard to understand.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.788 align:middle line:84%
So Haitian Creole speakers,
when they refer to men,

00:07:24.788 --> 00:07:26.330 align:middle line:84%
they're often
referring to Black men,

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:31.460 align:middle line:84%
since Haitian Creole
speakers tend to be Black.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:35.060 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, in Haitian Creole, I am
a "neg," although in French, I

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.760 align:middle line:90%
am not a "negre."

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:41.870 align:middle line:84%
Or similar
specialization of a term,

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:45.050 align:middle line:84%
there's the old English
word "chniht," which

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:48.620 align:middle line:84%
meant a servant, and has a
cognate in modern German,

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:53.840 align:middle line:84%
"knecht," which means something
like a boy or a servant.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.630 align:middle line:84%
It's the ancestor of the
modern English word "knight,"

00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:00.532 align:middle line:84%
who was originally specifically
a servant of the king.

00:08:00.532 --> 00:08:02.240 align:middle line:84%
The reason that the
English word "knight"

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.750 align:middle line:84%
has all of those
unpronounced letters in it

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.360 align:middle line:84%
is that those are there in an
attempt to pronounce the word

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.835 align:middle line:84%
or spell the word as
it was once pronounced.

00:08:11.835 --> 00:08:13.460 align:middle line:84%
We spell that with
a K at the beginning

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.200 align:middle line:84%
because it used to be
pronounced "cniht."

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.090 align:middle line:90%
There used to be a K there.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:20.780 align:middle line:84%
And then English got out of
the habit of pronouncing K

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.270 align:middle line:84%
before N, and so we have
words like "knight" and "knee"

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:29.240 align:middle line:84%
where the K is still there
as a memorial to the times

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.220 align:middle line:84%
when there was a stop
there at the beginning.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.960 align:middle line:84%
Same deal with the
G-H, which spelled

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.289 align:middle line:84%
a [HISSES IN BACK OF THROAT]
sound in the middle.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:39.080 align:middle line:90%
Or this is one of my favorites.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.880 align:middle line:84%
The old English
word "housewife,"

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.700 align:middle line:84%
"huswif," which is the ancestor
of the modern English word

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:44.870 align:middle line:90%
"hussy."

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:48.650 align:middle line:84%
That's undergone some semantic
change over the years,

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:51.650 align:middle line:84%
as it's gone down
through the years.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:56.210 align:middle line:84%
There's a disturbing
tendency for words

00:08:56.210 --> 00:09:01.610 align:middle line:84%
that refer to women to undergo
pejoration, that is, to become

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.065 align:middle line:90%
worse in their meaning.

00:09:03.065 --> 00:09:05.630 align:middle line:90%


00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:09.800 align:middle line:84%
There's possibly a
corresponding tendency.

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:12.290 align:middle line:84%
If you look at
lots of languages,

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:16.160 align:middle line:84%
there are lots of languages in
which the modern word for "man"

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:19.400 align:middle line:84%
is straightforwardly related
to earlier words for "man,"

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.050 align:middle line:84%
but in which the
word for "woman"

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:25.130 align:middle line:84%
has been replaced by some
other word for "woman."

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.070 align:middle line:84%
And I assume it's related
to this tendency, that words

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.343 align:middle line:84%
for women tend to become
insulting over time

00:09:31.343 --> 00:09:33.260 align:middle line:84%
in lots of different
cultures, and so you need

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.300 align:middle line:90%
to replace them with something.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:37.820 align:middle line:84%
Maybe relatedly, it's not
uncommon for modern words

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:41.740 align:middle line:84%
for women to originally refer
to queens or noblewomen.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.815 align:middle line:84%
That's true, for example,
both in German and in Zulu.

00:09:45.815 --> 00:09:48.065 align:middle line:84%
The modern Zulu word for
"woman" used to mean "queen."

00:09:48.065 --> 00:09:53.270 align:middle line:84%
It replaced an older word for
a woman that became insulting.

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:58.280 align:middle line:84%
Disturbing cross-linguistically
fairly common tendency.

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:00.980 align:middle line:84%
Or-- this is my actual
favorite in this list

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:04.280 align:middle line:84%
because I wonder
about the confusion

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:05.840 align:middle line:84%
that it must have
caused-- so there's

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.420 align:middle line:84%
a Proto-Austronesian
word reconstructed

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:11.370 align:middle line:84%
as "wada," which was an
existential construction.

00:10:11.370 --> 00:10:12.770 align:middle line:90%
It meant "there is."

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.170 align:middle line:84%
It has a nice,
straightforward descendant

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:18.170 align:middle line:84%
in Tagalog, "wala," which
means "there is not."

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.850 align:middle line:90%
I don't know how that happened.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:23.300 align:middle line:84%
There's an Arabic
word, "wala," which

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:25.280 align:middle line:90%
means something like "no."

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:26.960 align:middle line:84%
If you want to say
there are no books,

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.490 align:middle line:90%
you use "wala" to say that.

00:10:28.490 --> 00:10:31.350 align:middle line:84%
And it's possible that Tagalog
got influenced by Arabic.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.600 align:middle line:90%
It was in contact with Arabic.

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.457 align:middle line:90%
It has Arabic borrowings.

00:10:34.457 --> 00:10:36.290 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that's got
something to do with it.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:39.710 align:middle line:84%
But yes, there's presumably
a fairly weird period

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.870 align:middle line:84%
in the middle
there, where people

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:45.580 align:middle line:90%
were having existential crises.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.620 align:middle line:90%
I just used a star.

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:51.490 align:middle line:84%
You guys are used, by
now, to seeing stars

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.740 align:middle line:84%
on sentences that
are ungrammatical,

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:57.280 align:middle line:84%
and that is how stars
are used in syntax.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.620 align:middle line:84%
But when people are doing
historical linguistics,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.570 align:middle line:90%
stars mean something else.

00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:04.810 align:middle line:84%
They mean, this is a word
that we don't actually

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:09.020 align:middle line:84%
see in any documents,
or you can't hear it.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.310 align:middle line:84%
It's our theory about
an ancestral form.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.610 align:middle line:84%
So Proto-Austronesian
is the ancestor

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:20.050 align:middle line:84%
of a bunch of languages
of the Pacific, mostly.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.450 align:middle line:84%
So the languages of the
Philippines and Indonesia

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.940 align:middle line:90%
and New Zealand and Hawaii.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:28.180 align:middle line:84%
It's this gigantic geographic
area that they went over.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.630 align:middle line:84%
Plus some who got lost and
ended up in Madagascar.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:36.280 align:middle line:84%
There's an Austronesian language
over there, too, Malagasy.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.440 align:middle line:84%
The ancestor of all
of those languages,

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:40.000 align:middle line:90%
it's called Proto-Austronesian.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.880 align:middle line:84%
And that form that I just
showed you for "there is"

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:45.490 align:middle line:84%
is a reconstruction of what
they think that word would have

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:46.630 align:middle line:90%
been in Proto-Austronesian.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.400 align:middle line:84%
We haven't met any
Proto-Austronesians.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:50.200 align:middle line:84%
They didn't leave
us any documents.

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:53.470 align:middle line:84%
It's a hypothesized underlying
form, given all the forms

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:54.670 align:middle line:90%
that we can see.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.860 align:middle line:84%
That's what star means in
historical linguistics.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.420 align:middle line:90%


00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.130 align:middle line:84%
So various kinds
of semantic drift.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.990 align:middle line:84%
Recuttings-- so the example of
"misle" is an example of that.

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:08.390 align:middle line:84%
I saw the word
"misled," and I thought

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:12.080 align:middle line:84%
that it was the past tense
or the participle of a verb,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.330 align:middle line:90%
"to misle."

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.460 align:middle line:84%
There are other lots
of examples of this

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.400 align:middle line:84%
with the indefinite
article in English.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.670 align:middle line:84%
So the indefinite article
in English has two forms.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:27.990 align:middle line:84%
It's "an" before a vowel, and
it's "a" before a consonant.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:33.275 align:middle line:84%
So we say "an apple,"
but we say "a banana."

00:12:33.275 --> 00:12:36.740 align:middle line:90%


00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:37.460 align:middle line:90%
Yes, good.

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:39.780 align:middle line:84%
I always worry about when
to stop spelling banana.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.620 align:middle line:90%
It's not easy.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.440 align:middle line:84%
A consequence of that is that
if you hear a sequence like--

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.650 align:middle line:84%
this is one of the examples
for which this happened--

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.830 align:middle line:84%
you hear a sequence
like "a nickname,"

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:53.960 align:middle line:84%
it's sort of hard to know
whether you're hearing

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:56.990 align:middle line:90%
"a nickname" or "an ickname."

00:12:56.990 --> 00:13:00.740 align:middle line:84%
So "nickname" was originally
"ickname," "ekename."

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:05.120 align:middle line:90%
"Eke" is an old word for "also."

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:08.930 align:middle line:84%
It has a cognate in
German, which is "auch."

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:13.773 align:middle line:84%
It shows up in The
Canterbury Tales,

00:13:13.773 --> 00:13:15.440 align:middle line:84%
in the prologue to
The Canterbury Tales.

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:18.248 align:middle line:84%
If you ever read the prologue
to The Canterbury Tales,

00:13:18.248 --> 00:13:19.956 align:middle line:84%
Chaucer says something
like "and Zephyrus

00:13:19.956 --> 00:13:21.500 align:middle line:90%
eke, with his swete brethe"--

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:23.540 align:middle line:84%
"And Zephyr also with
his sweet breath."

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.130 align:middle line:90%
So that's what that "eke" is.

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:28.160 align:middle line:84%
It's an "also name," that is, a
name that you have in addition

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.740 align:middle line:90%
to your other name.

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:34.430 align:middle line:84%
If ordinary sound
changes had taken place,

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:37.070 align:middle line:84%
the word for "nickname"
would be "ickname,"

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:39.440 align:middle line:84%
but it's not, because
of confusion about where

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.050 align:middle line:90%
the N went, basically.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.150 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of words
like this in English.

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:47.460 align:middle line:90%
This is another example.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.200 align:middle line:84%
Middle English had a word
"pease" which was a mass noun.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:55.640 align:middle line:84%
By mass noun, I mean it referred
to a certain kind of stuff that

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:56.600 align:middle line:90%
didn't have a plural.

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:01.250 align:middle line:84%
We have a bunch of words
like this, words that

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:03.260 align:middle line:90%
don't ordinarily have plurals.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:09.770 align:middle line:84%
Words like "water" or
"ketchup" or "flour,"

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:14.750 align:middle line:84%
where if you have
a barrel of flour,

00:14:14.750 --> 00:14:18.278 align:middle line:84%
you don't talk about it as a
barrel of 16 million "flours."

00:14:18.278 --> 00:14:19.820 align:middle line:84%
That's not what
you're talking about.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:21.487 align:middle line:84%
You're talking about
a substance, right?

00:14:21.487 --> 00:14:23.120 align:middle line:90%
A stuff.

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:25.040 align:middle line:90%
Same deal with ketchup or water.

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:28.130 align:middle line:84%
You can talk about
different kinds of flours

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:29.750 align:middle line:90%
being different flours.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:32.030 align:middle line:84%
I guess if you were really,
really into ketchup,

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.030 align:middle line:84%
you could do the same
thing with ketchup, right?

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:37.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm fond of the ketchups that
you get in Southeast Asia,

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.640 align:middle line:90%
or whatever.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.130 align:middle line:84%
"Pease" used to be a
mass noun like that.

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:46.550 align:middle line:84%
It was just a name for this
green stuff that consists

00:14:46.550 --> 00:14:49.130 align:middle line:90%
of lots of little round things.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:52.790 align:middle line:84%
But because this
particular mass, well,

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:55.370 align:middle line:84%
can be subdivided into these
little individual, round,

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:58.160 align:middle line:84%
green things, and because
the word "pease" happened

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:00.830 align:middle line:84%
to end in a Z sound so
it looked like a plural,

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:02.600 align:middle line:90%
it got reanalyzed as a plural.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.300 align:middle line:84%
And so they made up
a singular "pea,"

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:07.168 align:middle line:90%
which didn't exist before.

00:15:07.168 --> 00:15:08.960 align:middle line:84%
English has done a lot
of things like this,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.820 align:middle line:84%
sometimes called
back- formation,

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:14.180 align:middle line:84%
where you take a word that looks
polymorphemic, although it's

00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:16.395 align:middle line:90%
not, and pretend that it is.

00:15:16.395 --> 00:15:18.770 align:middle line:84%
Another one, which I don't
think I have anywhere in here,

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.230 align:middle line:84%
is we had for a long
time a word, "beggar."

00:15:21.230 --> 00:15:23.750 align:middle line:90%


00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:26.480 align:middle line:84%
That noun was in the
language for a long time.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:31.550 align:middle line:84%
It ends in a sequence
that's pronounced schwa R--

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:35.570 align:middle line:84%
I've lost my ability to
write the IPA symbol for R--

00:15:35.570 --> 00:15:38.208 align:middle line:84%
which sounds like our
agentive suffix, the "-er"

00:15:38.208 --> 00:15:39.500 align:middle line:90%
that's at the end of "teacher."

00:15:39.500 --> 00:15:41.930 align:middle line:84%
And so people made
up a verb, "beg."

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:45.950 align:middle line:84%
But it wasn't originally
a polymorphemic word.

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:52.337 align:middle line:84%
That's one reason it's not
spelled "begger," which

00:15:52.337 --> 00:15:54.920 align:middle line:84%
is what you would have expected
if you were adding the teacher

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:56.962 align:middle line:90%
suffix to "beg."

00:15:56.962 --> 00:15:59.420 align:middle line:84%
It's spelled like this because,
well, this is not the "-er"

00:15:59.420 --> 00:15:59.920 align:middle line:90%
suffix.

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.580 align:middle line:90%
It's just pronounced like it.

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:07.700 align:middle line:84%
So the verb "to beg"
got back-formed.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:09.500 align:middle line:84%
Another similar
kind of recutting.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:12.770 align:middle line:84%
Here are three
Old English words.

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:17.040 align:middle line:84%
The positive form,
"near," which is "neah."

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:18.530 align:middle line:84%
Then that has a
comparative form,

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.150 align:middle line:90%
"nearer," which was "nearra."

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:22.190 align:middle line:84%
And then there was
a superlative form,

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:24.320 align:middle line:90%
"nearest," which was "neahsta."

00:16:24.320 --> 00:16:27.800 align:middle line:84%
All three of those words have
descendants in modern English.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:31.550 align:middle line:84%
"neah" is the ancestor of
"nigh," which we sort of still

00:16:31.550 --> 00:16:34.160 align:middle line:84%
have in modern English, as a
sort of archaic-sounding word

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:35.630 align:middle line:90%
for "near."

00:16:35.630 --> 00:16:37.640 align:middle line:90%
Don't use it so much anymore.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:40.340 align:middle line:84%
The comparative, the word
that meant "nearer," turned

00:16:40.340 --> 00:16:43.070 align:middle line:90%
into "near" in modern English.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:46.190 align:middle line:84%
And "neahsta," the
superlative, the nearest,

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:49.610 align:middle line:84%
is the ancestor of the
modern English word "next."

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:53.270 align:middle line:84%
Regular sound change applied
to "nearra" to give you "near"

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:56.990 align:middle line:84%
and obscured the fact
that it was a comparative.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:00.350 align:middle line:84%
So comparatives normally
end in a schwa-R sequence.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:02.120 align:middle line:84%
And because of
regular sound change,

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:04.993 align:middle line:84%
basically you were adding
the comparative suffix

00:17:04.993 --> 00:17:06.410 align:middle line:84%
to something that
ended in a vowel

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:08.385 align:middle line:84%
and then an H. The
consequence was

00:17:08.385 --> 00:17:10.760 align:middle line:84%
that you couldn't hear anymore
that it was a comparative,

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:12.365 align:middle line:84%
so it got reanalyzed
as something

00:17:12.365 --> 00:17:13.490 align:middle line:90%
that was not a comparative.

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:15.230 align:middle line:90%
So it's now our word for "near."

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:18.410 align:middle line:84%
It's more or less replaced
"nigh" as our word for "near."

00:17:18.410 --> 00:17:22.700 align:middle line:84%
And then we built new
comparatives and superlatives

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:23.240 align:middle line:90%
on that.

00:17:23.240 --> 00:17:25.047 align:middle line:90%
It was originally comparative.

00:17:25.047 --> 00:17:27.680 align:middle line:90%


00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:31.730 align:middle line:84%
All kinds of entertaining
things happen to languages,

00:17:31.730 --> 00:17:35.150 align:middle line:84%
sometimes as a
matter of somebody

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:39.020 align:middle line:84%
like me goes through life a
fairly long time believing

00:17:39.020 --> 00:17:39.980 align:middle line:90%
in a verb, "to misle."

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.620 align:middle line:84%
And if I had a more forceful
personality and more

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:45.140 align:middle line:84%
political clout,
then I might have

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:46.940 align:middle line:84%
been able to force
other people to believe

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:50.600 align:middle line:90%
in the verb "to misle" as well.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:53.420 align:middle line:84%
Some of this presumably
also is a matter

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:56.690 align:middle line:84%
of not misunderstandings,
but just people deliberately

00:17:56.690 --> 00:17:57.420 align:middle line:90%
being weird.

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:59.930 align:middle line:84%
I mean, if you think of things
that people do with language

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:02.990 align:middle line:84%
today, they have done this
all their lives, pretending

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:05.540 align:middle line:84%
that words mean things other
than what they mean just

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:08.180 align:middle line:84%
for fun, as a way of
having a good time

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:11.450 align:middle line:84%
or confusing your
parents or whatever else.

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:13.550 align:middle line:84%
The other big kind of
sound change, though,

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:15.300 align:middle line:84%
that I mainly want to
talk about today are

00:18:15.300 --> 00:18:17.840 align:middle line:90%
what are called sound changes.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:23.157 align:middle line:84%
Here are some numbers from
various languages of Europe.

00:18:23.157 --> 00:18:23.990 align:middle line:90%
Are they all Europe?

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:24.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, mostly.

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:25.480 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:26.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.420 align:middle line:90%
Europe, widely-- well, Sanskrit.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:30.560 align:middle line:90%
That's not in Europe.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.240 align:middle line:84%
So Sanskrit, Greek,
Latin, Gothic,

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:35.748 align:middle line:84%
which is an ancestor
of the Germanic.

00:18:35.748 --> 00:18:36.290 align:middle line:90%
No, it's not.

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:37.970 align:middle line:90%
It's an old Germanic language.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:41.420 align:middle line:90%
It was spoken around the Crimea.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.970 align:middle line:84%
Old Irish, Lithuanian,
Old Church Slavonic,

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:47.330 align:middle line:84%
which is an ancestor of
the Slavic languages.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:49.520 align:middle line:90%
Basque, and Turkish.

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:51.470 align:middle line:84%
If you look at these
languages, I've

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:53.960 align:middle line:84%
highlighted the second
line because it's

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:56.210 align:middle line:84%
the line in which
this is clearest.

00:18:56.210 --> 00:19:01.190 align:middle line:84%
You can see a lot of
these languages have words

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.910 align:middle line:84%
for the number two that look
kind of similar to each other

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:07.880 align:middle line:84%
except for the last two,
which really, really don't.

00:19:07.880 --> 00:19:11.660 align:middle line:84%
So you've got words
that have a "d"

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:14.180 align:middle line:84%
and then a "u," or
OK, Gothic has a "t,"

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:16.580 align:middle line:90%
the voiceless version of a "d."

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:18.800 align:middle line:84%
And then sometimes
there's a "w."

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:21.620 align:middle line:90%
Old Irish lost out on the "w."

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:23.420 align:middle line:84%
And then you get to
Basque and Turkish,

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:26.750 align:middle line:84%
where there's no "d" and no
"t" and nothing rounded at all.

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:27.660 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:30.240 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What does
the colon mean?

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.790 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, those
indicate long vowels.

00:19:32.790 --> 00:19:34.170 align:middle line:90%
Sorry.

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:34.840 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.460 align:middle line:90%
I should have said that.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:38.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:40.900 align:middle line:84%
Actually for Latin, I
should have used a macron.

00:19:40.900 --> 00:19:42.317 align:middle line:84%
I should go in
there and fix that.

00:19:42.317 --> 00:19:45.020 align:middle line:90%


00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:49.010 align:middle line:84%
So a hypothesis that people
could entertain, then--

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:53.810 align:middle line:84%
and this was famously
entertained for the first time

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.250 align:middle line:90%
a few centuries ago--

00:19:55.250 --> 00:20:00.230 align:middle line:84%
was that it's not just the case
that these various European

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:03.740 align:middle line:84%
languages have similar sounding
words for one, two, and three,

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:06.350 align:middle line:84%
but that these words are
similar sounding because they're

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:07.460 align:middle line:90%
related to each other.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.260 align:middle line:90%
The word for this is cognates.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.710 align:middle line:84%
These are all descendents
of a common ancestor.

00:20:12.710 --> 00:20:16.790 align:middle line:84%
And that Basque and Turkish
are not part of this group.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:19.290 align:middle line:84%
They don't have words that
are cognate with those.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:22.760 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why I specifically
boxed the words for three.

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:25.130 align:middle line:84%
The claim is that the
words for one and two

00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:28.310 align:middle line:84%
and three in most of those
languages in that table

00:20:28.310 --> 00:20:31.220 align:middle line:84%
are cognates with each other,
and that Basque and Turkish

00:20:31.220 --> 00:20:34.330 align:middle line:90%
are the ones that are left out.

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:37.170 align:middle line:84%
So I just said, hey,
look, these words look

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:38.430 align:middle line:90%
kind of similar to each other.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:41.358 align:middle line:84%
And that is indeed often how
this kind of thing starts.

00:20:41.358 --> 00:20:43.650 align:middle line:84%
You look at two languages
and say, hmm, these languages

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:44.525 align:middle line:90%
look kind of similar.

00:20:44.525 --> 00:20:46.470 align:middle line:90%
I wonder if they're related.

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:52.980 align:middle line:84%
But big discovery from the early
days of systematic linguistics

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.230 align:middle line:84%
was the observation
that it's possible to be

00:20:55.230 --> 00:20:57.580 align:middle line:90%
more systematic than this.

00:20:57.580 --> 00:21:01.200 align:middle line:84%
So it isn't just a matter
of saying, oh, look.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:03.790 align:middle line:84%
These languages look
similar to each other.

00:21:03.790 --> 00:21:06.810 align:middle line:84%
We can actually state
laws, generalizations,

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:10.518 align:middle line:84%
about which sounds correspond
to which other sounds.

00:21:10.518 --> 00:21:12.060 align:middle line:84%
So we can do better
than just saying,

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.380 align:middle line:90%
these languages look similar.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.290 align:middle line:84%
We get to say things
like, for example, where

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:19.320 align:middle line:84%
Latin and Greek
have a "d," English

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:23.050 align:middle line:84%
has a "t," for a
certain set of words.

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:29.460 align:middle line:84%
So the Latin and Greek words
for "two" and "eat" and "ten"

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:33.480 align:middle line:84%
have "d"s in them, which
correspond in English to "t"s.

00:21:33.480 --> 00:21:37.560 align:middle line:84%
Or the words in
Latin and Greek that

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:42.180 align:middle line:84%
are related to "kin" and
that second word means field.

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:46.740 align:middle line:84%
They have a "k" as corresponding
"k" sound in English.

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.350 align:middle line:84%
These words that
have a "b" in them--

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:52.380 align:middle line:84%
words that have a "b"
in them are weirdly rare

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:54.597 align:middle line:84%
in Proto-Indo-European,
the ancestor

00:21:54.597 --> 00:21:55.680 align:middle line:90%
of all of these languages.

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:57.510 align:middle line:84%
There are a couple
of examples here.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:22:00.840 align:middle line:84%
"Cannabis," the
second line there,

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:03.270 align:middle line:84%
is thought to probably
be a borrowing

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:05.820 align:middle line:84%
into Proto-Indo-European
from some other language

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:06.790 align:middle line:90%
for several reasons.

00:22:06.790 --> 00:22:08.540 align:middle line:84%
One is that it has a
"b" in it, and that's

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:09.810 align:middle line:90%
rare in Proto-Indo-European.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:11.880 align:middle line:84%
Another is that
it's weirdly long.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:13.650 align:middle line:84%
Proto-Indo-European
words are mostly not

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:15.270 align:middle line:90%
three syllables long.

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:20.160 align:middle line:84%
So a hypothesis is that
the ancestors of the Latins

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:23.310 align:middle line:84%
and the Romans and the Greeks
and the Sanskrit speakers

00:22:23.310 --> 00:22:26.670 align:middle line:84%
and the Gothic speakers,
the Proto-Indo-Europeans,

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:29.040 align:middle line:84%
as they were roaming
around Europe conquering

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:32.010 align:middle line:84%
various people, encountered
some people who had discovered

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:33.390 align:middle line:90%
cannabis and were like, cool.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:34.420 align:middle line:90%
We will take that.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:38.610 align:middle line:84%
And while we're at it, we
will borrow your word for it.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:40.980 align:middle line:84%
Apparently, something
like that happened.

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:43.590 align:middle line:84%
So there's this
generalization-- it's

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:46.110 align:middle line:84%
called Grimm's Law
because it was discovered

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:50.280 align:middle line:84%
by a Dane named Rasmus Rask
and then discovered again

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:54.930 align:middle line:84%
by Jacob Grimm, who was one of
the Grimm brothers of the Grimm

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:55.770 align:middle line:90%
Fairy Tales.

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:58.740 align:middle line:84%
This observation
about systematic sound

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:01.770 align:middle line:84%
correspondences between
the oldest versions

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:05.310 align:middle line:90%
of these related languages.

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:08.640 align:middle line:84%
You can see with the benefit
of all of the phonology

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.970 align:middle line:84%
that we did at the beginning of
this class that what's going on

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:15.640 align:middle line:84%
is that where Latin and
Greek have voiced stops,

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.270 align:middle line:84%
English and other
Germanic languages tend

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:19.860 align:middle line:90%
to have voiceless stops.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:22.790 align:middle line:84%
So there was a
general sound change.

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:24.540 align:middle line:84%
And there's more to
Grimm's Law than that,

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:26.340 align:middle line:84%
but that's one of
the observations

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.850 align:middle line:90%
that the Grimm made.

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.310 align:middle line:84%
Once we've looked at
a bunch of languages

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:35.310 align:middle line:84%
and figured out all the
sound laws that we need,

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.980 align:middle line:84%
we sort of get to posit
these underlying forms.

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.620 align:middle line:84%
I was talking about this
for Proto-Austronesian.

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:44.190 align:middle line:84%
These, you can think of them as
being like the underlying forms

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:46.680 align:middle line:84%
we talked about when we
did phonology, right?

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:49.530 align:middle line:84%
We posit these sound
changes, and we

00:23:49.530 --> 00:23:52.720 align:middle line:84%
have a sort of point of
origin for all of these words.

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:55.238 align:middle line:84%
It's the form that undergoes
different sound changes

00:23:55.238 --> 00:23:57.030 align:middle line:84%
in different languages
with the consequence

00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:58.770 align:middle line:84%
that you have
different languages.

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.500 align:middle line:84%
That underlying
form, the language

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:03.420 align:middle line:84%
that has those underlying
forms, is sometimes

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:04.780 align:middle line:90%
called the proto language.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:06.720 align:middle line:84%
So I've been talking
about Proto-Indo-European

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:10.260 align:middle line:90%
and Proto-Austronesian.

00:24:10.260 --> 00:24:13.320 align:middle line:84%
Here's one of the examples
of Grimm's Law at work.

00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:15.930 align:middle line:84%
We look at Sanskrit
and English and Latin.

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:18.700 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why I said
them in that order.

00:24:18.700 --> 00:24:22.290 align:middle line:84%
And we convince ourselves that
where Sanskrit and Latin have

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:25.170 align:middle line:90%
a "d," English has a "t."

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:29.250 align:middle line:84%
That's part of
Grimm's Law, so it

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:34.170 align:middle line:84%
has the consequence that voiced
sounds in Sanskrit and Latin

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:39.030 align:middle line:84%
correlate with voiceless
stops in English.

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:45.240 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the vowels, so
we have an "a" in Sanskrit

00:24:45.240 --> 00:24:47.790 align:middle line:90%
and an "e" in Latin.

00:24:47.790 --> 00:24:52.530 align:middle line:84%
And if we look more widely
at correlating words,

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:53.970 align:middle line:84%
words that look
like they might be

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:55.980 align:middle line:90%
related in Sanskrit and Latin--

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:59.130 align:middle line:90%
here are a bunch of examples.

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:01.320 align:middle line:84%
What kind of a
sound do you think

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:07.350 align:middle line:84%
we should posit as the proto
sound, a proto vowel for "eat"

00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:10.090 align:middle line:90%
in both Sanskrit and Latin?

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:13.810 align:middle line:84%
Have a look at those data
and tell me what you think.

00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:14.650 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [SNEEZE]

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:15.775 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Bless you.

00:25:15.775 --> 00:25:19.220 align:middle line:90%


00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:21.770 align:middle line:84%
Is there a generalization
that we can draw of the form

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:25.250 align:middle line:84%
wherever Sanskrit has an
"a," Latin has an "e"?

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:27.940 align:middle line:90%


00:25:27.940 --> 00:25:29.890 align:middle line:84%
That's true for
"eat" and "tooth."

00:25:29.890 --> 00:25:33.110 align:middle line:90%


00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:37.660 align:middle line:84%
And for the first
one in "field."

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:40.340 align:middle line:84%
But we're seeing that
for "sheep" and "two"

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:42.310 align:middle line:84%
and for the second
vowel in "field,"

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:44.170 align:middle line:84%
there are other Latin
vowels that correspond

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.790 align:middle line:90%
with Sanskrit vowels.

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:49.000 align:middle line:84%
So what type of
a vowel should we

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:51.145 align:middle line:84%
posited as the original
vowel in "eat"?

00:25:51.145 --> 00:25:55.750 align:middle line:90%


00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:57.430 align:middle line:84%
Can we predict the
Sanskrit vowels--

00:25:57.430 --> 00:25:59.740 align:middle line:84%
or sorry, the Latin vowels
from the Sanskrit vowels?

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:02.600 align:middle line:90%


00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:03.100 align:middle line:90%
No.

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:03.400 align:middle line:90%
Right?

00:26:03.400 --> 00:26:04.817 align:middle line:84%
There aren't
generalizations like,

00:26:04.817 --> 00:26:08.240 align:middle line:84%
wherever Sanskrit has
an "a," Latin as an "e."

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:08.990 align:middle line:90%
What should we do?

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:10.150 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:26:10.150 --> 00:26:12.593 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: So we [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:12.593 --> 00:26:13.510 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:16.990 align:middle line:84%
So if we do it the
other way around,

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:21.070 align:middle line:84%
if we say the original
vowels were more

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:22.750 align:middle line:84%
like the Latin
vowels, and Sanskrit

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.420 align:middle line:84%
underwent a bunch of sound
changes that squashed many

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:26.380 align:middle line:90%
of the vowels together.

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:32.710 align:middle line:84%
So the original vowels
"e" and "o" and also "a,"

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:36.100 align:middle line:84%
like the first vowel
in "field," all three

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:40.750 align:middle line:84%
of those vowels became
"a," "ah," in Sanskrit.

00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:42.310 align:middle line:84%
This is the same
kind of reasoning

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:44.590 align:middle line:84%
we were using when we were
originally doing phonology.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:48.043 align:middle line:84%
We can't predict the Latin
vowels from the Sanskrit

00:26:48.043 --> 00:26:49.960 align:middle line:84%
vowels, but we can predict
the Sanskrit vowels

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:50.770 align:middle line:90%
from the Latin vowels.

00:26:50.770 --> 00:26:52.895 align:middle line:84%
Namely, the Latin vowels
all got squashed together.

00:26:52.895 --> 00:26:56.530 align:middle line:84%
So Latin has kept an
older vowel system

00:26:56.530 --> 00:27:00.940 align:middle line:84%
that Sanskrit has simplified
to a certain extent.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:04.560 align:middle line:84%
So we'll posit a
Proto-Indo-European stem, "ed-"

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:06.000 align:middle line:90%
for "eat."

00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:10.380 align:middle line:84%
Sanskrit undergoes a change,
where the "eh" becomes "ah."

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.390 align:middle line:84%
English undergoes
Grimm's Law and also

00:27:12.390 --> 00:27:14.640 align:middle line:84%
some changes with the vowels,
which we'll get a chance

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:16.200 align:middle line:90%
to talk about in the second.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:19.170 align:middle line:84%
And Latin preserves
at least that much

00:27:19.170 --> 00:27:21.540 align:middle line:90%
of the Proto-Indo-European form.

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:22.920 align:middle line:90%
It's worth being careful.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.010 align:middle line:84%
Just like at the beginning
of this class when

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:30.930 align:middle line:84%
we were doing phonology, we
started off with sound changes,

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:33.720 align:middle line:84%
looking at data sets,
where it was often the case

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:37.620 align:middle line:84%
that you could posit
an underlying form that

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:42.690 align:middle line:84%
was the same as some form that
you could see on the surface.

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:45.510 align:middle line:84%
But I think we saw, back
when we were doing phonology,

00:27:45.510 --> 00:27:47.200 align:middle line:90%
that wasn't necessarily true.

00:27:47.200 --> 00:27:49.890 align:middle line:84%
There were cases where it was
useful to posit an underlying

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:54.000 align:middle line:84%
form that always underwent
some kind of change.

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.910 align:middle line:84%
It never just surfaced
in its actual form.

00:27:56.910 --> 00:28:00.000 align:middle line:84%
And this also happens when
we're positing proto forms.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.910 align:middle line:84%
It hasn't happened in this
particular case, so this--

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:05.580 align:middle line:84%
I carefully picked a case
where life was simple,

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:08.640 align:middle line:84%
and Latin has just kept the
Proto-Indo-European form.

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:10.660 align:middle line:84%
But we can't rely on that
happening every time.

00:28:10.660 --> 00:28:13.118 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes there are cases where
we'll want a positive proto

00:28:13.118 --> 00:28:16.320 align:middle line:84%
form that just doesn't
survive in its unchanged

00:28:16.320 --> 00:28:17.940 align:middle line:90%
form in any case.

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.540 align:middle line:90%


00:28:20.540 --> 00:28:22.910 align:middle line:90%
Sound changes.

00:28:22.910 --> 00:28:24.830 align:middle line:90%
The study of sound changes--

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:28.340 align:middle line:84%
first of all, the discovery
that sound changes were regular,

00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:30.170 align:middle line:84%
that you could make
statements of the form

00:28:30.170 --> 00:28:32.090 align:middle line:84%
wherever this
language has sound X,

00:28:32.090 --> 00:28:34.820 align:middle line:84%
this other language
has sound Y, that

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:39.200 align:middle line:84%
was a big early discovery in
linguistics and the starting

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:42.830 align:middle line:84%
point for a lot of the work
that started getting done

00:28:42.830 --> 00:28:45.890 align:middle line:84%
in phonology as
people started getting

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:48.440 align:middle line:84%
interested in the questions
of what kinds of sound changes

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:51.050 align:middle line:84%
there were and why some
kinds of sound changes

00:28:51.050 --> 00:28:54.200 align:middle line:84%
were more common than others
and what motivated them

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:56.400 align:middle line:90%
and why they happened and so on.

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:58.550 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, the
discussion that we

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:00.920 align:middle line:84%
had when we were doing
phonology rule ordering,

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:05.183 align:middle line:84%
where we decided that it was
useful sometimes to posit--

00:29:05.183 --> 00:29:07.100 align:middle line:84%
we talked about this for
Lardil, for example--

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:08.630 align:middle line:84%
that it's useful
sometimes to posit

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.790 align:middle line:84%
an original form,
an underlying form,

00:29:10.790 --> 00:29:14.150 align:middle line:84%
and have it undergo sound
changes in a particular order.

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:16.860 align:middle line:84%
I think we talked about it as
being like an assembly line,

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:17.360 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:20.340 align:middle line:84%
It first goes to the place where
it undergoes this sound change,

00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:22.700 align:middle line:84%
then it undergoes the sound
change, with the consequence

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:26.090 align:middle line:84%
sometimes that the second
sound change creates something

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:27.920 align:middle line:84%
that would undergo the
first sound change,

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:28.910 align:middle line:90%
but it's too late.

00:29:28.910 --> 00:29:30.470 align:middle line:90%
We looked at examples like that.

00:29:30.470 --> 00:29:34.550 align:middle line:90%


00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:37.490 align:middle line:84%
Way back when we were
talking about Lardil,

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:41.780 align:middle line:84%
we had a rule that
changed "u" to "a"

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:44.510 align:middle line:84%
at the ends of
words that changed

00:29:44.510 --> 00:29:47.270 align:middle line:84%
underlying "muwu," which is the
underlying word for "water,"

00:29:47.270 --> 00:29:51.350 align:middle line:90%
to "muga."

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:55.160 align:middle line:84%
And we had another sound
change that got rid of "k'

00:29:55.160 --> 00:29:57.303 align:middle line:90%
at the ends of words.

00:29:57.303 --> 00:29:59.720 align:middle line:84%
So if you had a "k" at the end
of the word, it would drop,

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:02.000 align:middle line:84%
which changed
underlying "naluk,"

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:07.492 align:middle line:84%
which is the word for
"story," to "nalu,"

00:30:07.492 --> 00:30:09.950 align:middle line:84%
But it was important that these
rules applied in this order

00:30:09.950 --> 00:30:13.000 align:middle line:84%
because "nalu" doesn't
then change to "nala."

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:14.542 align:middle line:84%
So that was one of
the kinds of cases

00:30:14.542 --> 00:30:16.917 align:middle line:84%
we were talking about when we
were talking about ordering

00:30:16.917 --> 00:30:18.140 align:middle line:90%
rules in a particular order.

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:22.310 align:middle line:84%
All of that talk started in
work on historical linguistics,

00:30:22.310 --> 00:30:25.310 align:middle line:84%
where it's easy to
imagine, in fact,

00:30:25.310 --> 00:30:27.440 align:middle line:84%
that a bunch of sound
changes have taken place,

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:29.360 align:middle line:84%
and they've taken place
in a particular order.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.630 align:middle line:84%
And people got interested
in possible relations

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:35.048 align:middle line:90%
between sound changes.

00:30:35.048 --> 00:30:36.590 align:middle line:84%
Some kinds of sound
changes are cross

00:30:36.590 --> 00:30:38.010 align:middle line:90%
linguistically fairly common.

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:39.470 align:middle line:90%
So here's one.

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:43.470 align:middle line:84%
"w" changed to "gwa,"
for example, in Chamorro.

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:49.620 align:middle line:84%
Chamorro is a language
of Saipan in the Pacific.

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:53.025 align:middle line:84%
It's closely related
to Tagalog, which is

00:30:53.025 --> 00:30:54.275 align:middle line:90%
a language of the Philippines.

00:30:54.275 --> 00:30:56.533 align:middle line:84%
They're both
Austronesian languages.

00:30:56.533 --> 00:30:57.950 align:middle line:84%
So for example,
Tagalog has a word

00:30:57.950 --> 00:30:59.790 align:middle line:90%
"asawa," which means "spouse."

00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:03.320 align:middle line:84%
So Tagalog kinship
terminology mostly

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:05.060 align:middle line:84%
doesn't indicate the
gender of the person

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:05.940 align:middle line:90%
you're talking about.

00:31:05.940 --> 00:31:08.540 align:middle line:84%
So they don't have words
for husband or wife.

00:31:08.540 --> 00:31:11.660 align:middle line:84%
They just have this word
"asawa," which means spouse.

00:31:11.660 --> 00:31:14.810 align:middle line:84%
You can say male spouse
or female spouse,

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:18.530 align:middle line:90%
but you don't, usually.

00:31:18.530 --> 00:31:20.210 align:middle line:90%
So Tagalog has a word, "asawa."

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.070 align:middle line:90%
In Chamorro, this is "asagwa."

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:23.630 align:middle line:90%
So the "w" has become a "gwa."

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.870 align:middle line:84%
Tagalog has a number, "dalawa,"
which is the number two.

00:31:26.870 --> 00:31:30.080 align:middle line:84%
That corresponds to
"hugwa" in Chamorro,

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:32.430 align:middle line:84%
and this "wala," which we
were talking about before,

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:34.610 align:middle line:84%
which means "there
isn't" just in Tagalog.

00:31:34.610 --> 00:31:37.790 align:middle line:84%
All over Austronesian means
"there is," and that's

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:38.930 align:middle line:90%
what it means in Chamorro.

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:40.847 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't start
with the "w" in Chamorro,

00:31:40.847 --> 00:31:42.740 align:middle line:90%
it starts with "gwa."

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:45.110 align:middle line:84%
If you think about
what a "w" is,

00:31:45.110 --> 00:31:48.080 align:middle line:84%
the fact that a "w"
becomes a "gwa" in Chamorro

00:31:48.080 --> 00:31:51.740 align:middle line:90%
is maybe not such a weird fact.

00:31:51.740 --> 00:31:53.030 align:middle line:90%
So think about what a "w" is.

00:31:53.030 --> 00:31:58.350 align:middle line:84%
A "w" is a glide that
corresponds to the vowel "u."

00:31:58.350 --> 00:32:01.800 align:middle line:84%
If you think back, way,
way back, months ago,

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:06.030 align:middle line:84%
when you were young and carefree
and knew a lot about phonology

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:10.320 align:middle line:84%
and had not yet encountered
syntax and semantics,

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:14.370 align:middle line:84%
maybe you remember that the
value "u" has rounding, right?

00:32:14.370 --> 00:32:15.540 align:middle line:90%
Your lips are rounding.

00:32:15.540 --> 00:32:18.400 align:middle line:90%
But it's also high and back.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:20.730 align:middle line:84%
What that means is that
your tongue is bunched up

00:32:20.730 --> 00:32:22.800 align:middle line:84%
toward the top of the
back of your mouth.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:27.190 align:middle line:84%
So back then, we were doing
group exercises like saying

00:32:27.190 --> 00:32:29.620 align:middle line:90%
"oo-wee" over and over again.

00:32:29.620 --> 00:32:32.547 align:middle line:84%
And when you did
that, you discovered

00:32:32.547 --> 00:32:34.380 align:middle line:84%
that your lips were
rounding and unrounding,

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:35.880 align:middle line:84%
but also that your
tongue was shifting

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:38.172 align:middle line:84%
from the back of your mouth
to the front of your mouth.

00:32:38.172 --> 00:32:42.600 align:middle line:84%
So "w" is a glide that
corresponds to the vowel "u."

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:46.560 align:middle line:84%
It's a version of the vowel
"u" said very fast, basically,

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.415 align:middle line:84%
in which your tongue
is bunching up

00:32:48.415 --> 00:32:50.040 align:middle line:84%
toward the back of
your mouth, reaching

00:32:50.040 --> 00:32:51.748 align:middle line:84%
toward the top of the
back of your mouth,

00:32:51.748 --> 00:32:53.650 align:middle line:90%
and your lips are rounding.

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:58.396 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about what you
do when you do a "g," a "guh,"

00:32:58.396 --> 00:33:01.300 align:middle line:84%
a voiced velar stop,
well what you're doing

00:33:01.300 --> 00:33:04.480 align:middle line:84%
is your tongue is touching
the back of your mouth, right?

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:07.520 align:middle line:84%
It's making a velar
closure back there.

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:11.260 align:middle line:84%
So the fact that a "wa" has
become a "gwa" in this language

00:33:11.260 --> 00:33:12.570 align:middle line:90%
is sort of understandable.

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:15.850 align:middle line:84%
A "gwa" is just a "w" that
got out of hand, right?

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:18.880 align:middle line:84%
It's your tongue
not just bunching up

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:22.300 align:middle line:84%
toward the back of your mouth,
but actually colliding with it,

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:25.540 align:middle line:90%
making a closure back there.

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:28.150 align:middle line:84%
And I'm going through all
this because this sound change

00:33:28.150 --> 00:33:31.090 align:middle line:90%
didn't just happen in Chamorro.

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:34.090 align:middle line:90%
It also happened in Welsh.

00:33:34.090 --> 00:33:36.520 align:middle line:90%
So Welsh has a bunch of words.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.680 align:middle line:84%
I've just given you
one, which is "man."

00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:40.480 align:middle line:84%
The Welsh word for
"man" is "gwir."

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.897 align:middle line:84%
It's related to the Latin word
"vir," [pronounced "weer"],

00:33:42.897 --> 00:33:45.580 align:middle line:84%
which we have as "virile"
and English words like that.

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:49.120 align:middle line:84%
The "vir" is actually also, I
think, related to the "were"

00:33:49.120 --> 00:33:51.880 align:middle line:84%
in "werewolf," which
is a man, a man-wolf.

00:33:51.880 --> 00:33:54.550 align:middle line:90%


00:33:54.550 --> 00:33:57.820 align:middle line:84%
Welsh has another word
which they borrowed from--

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:00.460 align:middle line:90%


00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:04.960 align:middle line:84%
borrowed from Latin,
"gwin," which is "wine."

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:06.460 align:middle line:84%
So they borrowed
that from Latin.

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:09.100 align:middle line:84%
They changed the "w" sound
at the beginning to a "gwa."

00:34:09.100 --> 00:34:11.139 align:middle line:84%
So Latin underwent--
Welsh, sorry,

00:34:11.139 --> 00:34:13.052 align:middle line:90%
underwent the same sound change.

00:34:13.052 --> 00:34:14.469 align:middle line:84%
There are also
some words that got

00:34:14.469 --> 00:34:18.489 align:middle line:84%
borrowed from Proto-Germanic
into late Latin, that

00:34:18.489 --> 00:34:21.130 align:middle line:84%
is, Latin right before it was
about to break up and turn

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:24.639 align:middle line:84%
into all the Romance languages,
in which the Proto-Germanic

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:27.580 align:middle line:84%
"w" got realized in
late Latin as a "gwa."

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:30.864 align:middle line:84%
So the Proto-Germanic word
for "war," which was "werra"--

00:34:30.864 --> 00:34:34.870 align:middle line:84%
it's an ancestor of the
modern English, "war"--

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:38.980 align:middle line:84%
got borrowed into late
Latin as "gwerra."

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:43.929 align:middle line:84%
And you now see that in
English words like guerrilla,

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:47.132 align:middle line:84%
in the sense of a
person who fights

00:34:47.132 --> 00:34:48.340 align:middle line:90%
a particular type of warfare.

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:51.250 align:middle line:90%


00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:54.010 align:middle line:84%
If you study any
Romance languages,

00:34:54.010 --> 00:35:00.730 align:middle line:84%
the old Latin word for
"war" was "bellum."

00:35:00.730 --> 00:35:03.010 align:middle line:84%
But that word, I
believe, vanished

00:35:03.010 --> 00:35:04.420 align:middle line:90%
from all the Romance languages.

00:35:04.420 --> 00:35:07.370 align:middle line:84%
What they all have are
descendants of this word.

00:35:07.370 --> 00:35:12.760 align:middle line:84%
So if you study Italian
or French or Spanish,

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:16.660 align:middle line:84%
you need to learn words for
war like "guerre" and "guerra."

00:35:16.660 --> 00:35:18.980 align:middle line:84%
Those are all descendants
of this Germanic word.

00:35:18.980 --> 00:35:20.890 align:middle line:84%
So the late Latins,
I guess, were really

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:22.960 align:middle line:84%
impressed with the Germanic
attitude toward war.

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:25.120 align:middle line:84%
They borrowed this word,
and they completely

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:27.673 align:middle line:84%
replaced their word for "war"
with it, as far as I can tell.

00:35:27.673 --> 00:35:29.590 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do we know why
"bellum" disappeared?

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:30.270 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: No.

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:30.960 align:middle line:90%
At least, I don't.

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:31.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:34.200 align:middle line:84%
This just became the
cool word for war.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:36.630 align:middle line:84%
So I mean, we have
it in expressions

00:35:36.630 --> 00:35:39.150 align:middle line:84%
that we've borrowed from
Latin, like "antebellum."

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:43.260 align:middle line:84%
But as far as their
normal word for war,

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.540 align:middle line:90%
it got replaced with this.

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Or we also have words
in English where

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:49.120 align:middle line:90%
we have both of these words.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:50.820 align:middle line:84%
So there's the
Proto-Germanic word

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:52.340 align:middle line:90%
for "guard," which was "ward."

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:55.170 align:middle line:84%
We have that as a word,
"ward," to ward off a threat

00:35:55.170 --> 00:35:56.940 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:59.610 align:middle line:84%
It got borrowed into
late Latin as "gward."

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:04.030 align:middle line:84%
That's the modern word for
"watch" in lots of languages.

00:36:04.030 --> 00:36:06.660 align:middle line:84%
So in Italian, if
you want to watch TV,

00:36:06.660 --> 00:36:09.000 align:middle line:90%
the verb you use is "guardare."

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.200 align:middle line:90%
You guard the TV.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:11.310 align:middle line:90%
You watch it carefully.

00:36:11.310 --> 00:36:13.980 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:16.920 align:middle line:84%
And English has both of these
words now, so we have "ward"

00:36:16.920 --> 00:36:18.210 align:middle line:90%
and we also have "guard."

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:20.520 align:middle line:90%
They both have the same root.

00:36:20.520 --> 00:36:22.560 align:middle line:84%
They're both from
Proto-Germanic "ward,"

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:25.350 align:middle line:84%
which came to us straight
in the form of "ward,"

00:36:25.350 --> 00:36:28.110 align:middle line:84%
and then took a little detour
into Latin to become "guard."

00:36:28.110 --> 00:36:31.110 align:middle line:84%
And then we borrowed it
again in a different form.

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:34.740 align:middle line:90%


00:36:34.740 --> 00:36:36.000 align:middle line:90%
Big discovery, then.

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:38.010 align:middle line:84%
Grimm's Law, this
change from "w"

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:42.323 align:middle line:84%
to "gwa," so lots
of big discoveries.

00:36:42.323 --> 00:36:43.740 align:middle line:84%
But one of the big
discoveries was

00:36:43.740 --> 00:36:46.032 align:middle line:84%
that sound change was regular,
that you could do better

00:36:46.032 --> 00:36:49.647 align:middle line:84%
than just saying these
words look kind of similar

00:36:49.647 --> 00:36:50.230 align:middle line:90%
to each other.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:51.690 align:middle line:84%
I bet these languages
are related.

00:36:51.690 --> 00:36:53.160 align:middle line:84%
You could actually
say, oh, yeah.

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:54.810 align:middle line:84%
These words are--
these languages

00:36:54.810 --> 00:36:58.800 align:middle line:84%
are related because there is
this general law about how

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:01.650 align:middle line:84%
sounds in language X are
related to sounds in language Y.

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:04.680 align:middle line:84%
This was called the
Neogrammarian Hypothesis,

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:06.720 align:middle line:84%
and it was one of the
big, early discoveries.

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:10.230 align:middle line:84%
It shifts emphasis
away from looking

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:12.752 align:middle line:84%
at lists of words that
look kind of similar.

00:37:12.752 --> 00:37:14.460 align:middle line:84%
What we're looking
for are lists of words

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:17.043 align:middle line:84%
that can be related by regular
sound change laws, which is not

00:37:17.043 --> 00:37:18.040 align:middle line:90%
the same thing.

00:37:18.040 --> 00:37:19.670 align:middle line:84%
There could be lots
of sound changes.

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:21.420 align:middle line:84%
The sound changes could
be really radical.

00:37:21.420 --> 00:37:23.087 align:middle line:84%
The result could be
that the words don't

00:37:23.087 --> 00:37:24.430 align:middle line:90%
look like each other at all.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:26.280 align:middle line:84%
The point is to
discover that there

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:29.310 align:middle line:84%
are regular correspondences
between the words

00:37:29.310 --> 00:37:30.000 align:middle line:90%
and the lists.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:31.120 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:34.840 align:middle line:84%
That's how you discover that
two languages are related.

00:37:34.840 --> 00:37:37.530 align:middle line:84%
So just to give you a
dramatic example of this,

00:37:37.530 --> 00:37:39.630 align:middle line:84%
here are three
words for the number

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:44.160 align:middle line:84%
two in three languages
whose names I have cunningly

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.248 align:middle line:90%
hidden from you.

00:37:46.248 --> 00:37:48.540 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them language A,
language B, and language C.

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:50.332 align:middle line:84%
And I will tell you
that two of these words

00:37:50.332 --> 00:37:53.590 align:middle line:84%
are related to each other
and the third is not.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:55.690 align:middle line:84%
You're probably
suspecting a trick.

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:58.540 align:middle line:84%
But go ahead,
somebody fall for it.

00:37:58.540 --> 00:38:00.190 align:middle line:84%
Which of these two
languages do you

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:01.880 align:middle line:90%
think are related to each other?

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:02.380 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:38:02.380 --> 00:38:03.160 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: B and C.

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:05.202 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: B and C.
Yes, see, you're right.

00:38:05.202 --> 00:38:08.500 align:middle line:84%
But that's because you know
how this kind of thing works.

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:10.960 align:middle line:84%
You're supposed to say A and
B. Would somebody go ahead

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:11.740 align:middle line:90%
and say A and B?

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:12.730 align:middle line:90%
I would feel better.

00:38:12.730 --> 00:38:13.480 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Is it A and B?

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:16.063 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, yes, it's
A and B. Excellent suggestion.

00:38:16.063 --> 00:38:17.380 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:19.780 align:middle line:90%
But of course, he's right.

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:25.330 align:middle line:84%
It is B and C. "Er" Is
the Mandarin word for two.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.310 align:middle line:84%
"Erku" is the
Armenian word for two,

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:31.120 align:middle line:84%
and Greek has "duo"
as it's word for two.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:33.730 align:middle line:84%
And the way you convince
yourself of this

00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:37.060 align:middle line:84%
is by discovering that
wherever Greek has a "dwa,"

00:38:37.060 --> 00:38:40.893 align:middle line:84%
Armenian has an "erk," which is
a pretty radical sound change.

00:38:40.893 --> 00:38:42.310 align:middle line:84%
It took people a
while to discover

00:38:42.310 --> 00:38:44.800 align:middle line:84%
that Armenian was an
Indo-European language

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:48.082 align:middle line:84%
because Armenian has had
really radical things happen

00:38:48.082 --> 00:38:50.290 align:middle line:84%
to it, with the consequence
that its words don't look

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:52.330 align:middle line:90%
all that Indo-European anymore.

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:54.970 align:middle line:84%
But it underwent this
general sound change,

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.715 align:middle line:84%
and the point is you discover
that the sound change is

00:38:57.715 --> 00:38:59.590 align:middle line:84%
general, and that's how
you convince yourself

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:01.510 align:middle line:90%
that it belongs to this club.

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:02.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:02.050 --> 00:39:02.800 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Why?

00:39:02.800 --> 00:39:05.330 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Why did it do that?

00:39:05.330 --> 00:39:09.160 align:middle line:84%
So there isn't any really
good answer to that,

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:10.840 align:middle line:90%
but you could think.

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:13.420 align:middle line:84%
If you imagined first
that they decided

00:39:13.420 --> 00:39:17.907 align:middle line:84%
that it wasn't good to have
words start with "d" and "w."

00:39:17.907 --> 00:39:19.990 align:middle line:84%
Like, that's too many
consonants at the beginning.

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:20.830 align:middle line:90%
We don't want that.

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:22.600 align:middle line:90%
We're going to add a vowel.

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:25.750 align:middle line:84%
So they'll add a vowel
at the beginning.

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:28.960 align:middle line:84%
This alternation
between "d" and "r"

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:32.420 align:middle line:84%
is not such a
strange thing to do.

00:39:32.420 --> 00:39:34.990 align:middle line:90%
So an "r" is kind of--

00:39:34.990 --> 00:39:38.710 align:middle line:84%
it's not unlike the relationship
between "w" and "u",

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:42.700 align:middle line:84%
that "d" is an actual stop,
an "ruh," it is unlike that.

00:39:42.700 --> 00:39:45.940 align:middle line:84%
"d" is an actual stop, and
"r," especially if it's a flap,

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:48.550 align:middle line:84%
is you're just sort of
whacking the part of your mouth

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:50.680 align:middle line:84%
that where you
make the "d" stop.

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:53.440 align:middle line:84%
And then the fact that
"w" became a "k," well,

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:59.320 align:middle line:84%
we've seen "w" becoming a
"gwa" in Welsh and in Chamorro.

00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:03.760 align:middle line:84%
And a "k" has a stop in a
place where a "w" would just

00:40:03.760 --> 00:40:04.690 align:middle line:90%
be an approximant.

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:07.900 align:middle line:84%
So it's maybe not a completely
crazy set of sound changes.

00:40:07.900 --> 00:40:10.540 align:middle line:84%
I should say, it's not
always possible to understand

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:13.600 align:middle line:84%
why a language underwent the
sound changes that it did.

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:14.843 align:middle line:90%
There's a beautiful example.

00:40:14.843 --> 00:40:17.260 align:middle line:84%
I have sometimes used this as
a problem set in this class.

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:18.670 align:middle line:84%
If you were going to
have another problem set,

00:40:18.670 --> 00:40:19.920 align:middle line:90%
it might have been about this.

00:40:19.920 --> 00:40:25.523 align:middle line:84%
There's an Algonquian
language, Arapaho.

00:40:25.523 --> 00:40:27.190 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
Algonquian languages,

00:40:27.190 --> 00:40:29.230 align:middle line:84%
the Proto-Algonquian
can be reconstructed

00:40:29.230 --> 00:40:33.370 align:middle line:90%
to a fair degree of confidence.

00:40:33.370 --> 00:40:35.167 align:middle line:84%
There are a bunch of
Algonquian languages.

00:40:35.167 --> 00:40:36.250 align:middle line:90%
They're very well studied.

00:40:36.250 --> 00:40:37.250 align:middle line:90%
There are sound changes.

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:39.490 align:middle line:84%
It's not all that hard to
relate them to each other.

00:40:39.490 --> 00:40:42.310 align:middle line:84%
Arapaho is an Algonquian
language, but it has undergone

00:40:42.310 --> 00:40:45.070 align:middle line:90%
sound changes like--

00:40:45.070 --> 00:40:49.510 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to remember them
all. "p" has become "chuh."

00:40:49.510 --> 00:40:52.780 align:middle line:84%
"m" has become--
what has "m" become?

00:40:52.780 --> 00:40:56.320 align:middle line:90%
"m" has become "b."

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:58.990 align:middle line:84%
There's a sound-- so
with the consequence

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:01.030 align:middle line:84%
that-- oh, no. "m"
has become "chuh."

00:41:01.030 --> 00:41:03.910 align:middle line:84%
They've both become "chuh."
they've blended together.

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:05.890 align:middle line:84%
So the consequence
is that the word

00:41:05.890 --> 00:41:08.320 align:middle line:84%
for "dog," which is the
one that I can remember off

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:10.390 align:middle line:90%
the top of my head.

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:14.650 align:middle line:84%
There's a word for "dog"
that in Wampanoag, which is

00:41:14.650 --> 00:41:17.320 align:middle line:90%
spoken around here, is "anum."

00:41:17.320 --> 00:41:18.280 align:middle line:90%
This is a schwa.

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:27.410 align:middle line:84%
And in Passamaquoddy is
"alum," and in Cree is "ateem."

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:31.030 align:middle line:84%
And so there's an "m'
here that corresponds,

00:41:31.030 --> 00:41:33.280 align:middle line:84%
and there are vowels
here that have undergone

00:41:33.280 --> 00:41:34.630 align:middle line:90%
various kinds of sound changes.

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:36.960 align:middle line:84%
There's a sound here, which
is kind of mysterious.

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:40.600 align:middle line:84%
It's unclear how to represent
it in Proto-Algonquian.

00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:42.940 align:middle line:90%
But so here's one Wampanoag.

00:41:42.940 --> 00:41:45.910 align:middle line:90%
Here's Passamaquoddy.

00:41:45.910 --> 00:41:48.310 align:middle line:90%
Here's Cree.

00:41:48.310 --> 00:41:54.910 align:middle line:84%
And the Arapaho word
for it is "fench."

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:55.450 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:42:00.310 align:middle line:84%
So you've got "anum," "alum,"
"ateem" and then "fench"

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:01.670 align:middle line:90%
is the Arapaho word.

00:42:01.670 --> 00:42:05.230 align:middle line:84%
So the Arapahos, I don't
know what happened to them.

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:08.050 align:middle line:84%
But they underwent sound
changes that it's very hard

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:09.700 align:middle line:90%
to justify to yourself.

00:42:09.700 --> 00:42:11.470 align:middle line:90%
It's unclear what happened.

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:14.110 align:middle line:84%
I was once talking with
a linguist about this,

00:42:14.110 --> 00:42:16.570 align:middle line:84%
and his theory was
that what happened

00:42:16.570 --> 00:42:20.950 align:middle line:84%
with the Arapaho was that
they became a horse culture.

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:23.080 align:middle line:84%
When horses were
brought to the US,

00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:25.090 align:middle line:84%
the Arapaho got
really into horses.

00:42:25.090 --> 00:42:27.790 align:middle line:84%
And his theory was
there must have

00:42:27.790 --> 00:42:30.550 align:middle line:84%
been a generation of Arapaho
who just sort of stopped

00:42:30.550 --> 00:42:33.718 align:middle line:84%
listening to their parents,
It underwent all kinds

00:42:33.718 --> 00:42:35.260 align:middle line:84%
of radical sound
changes because they

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:36.820 align:middle line:90%
were too busy riding horses.

00:42:36.820 --> 00:42:40.450 align:middle line:90%


00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:42.320 align:middle line:90%
This is an example where--

00:42:42.320 --> 00:42:42.820 align:middle line:90%
thank you.

00:42:42.820 --> 00:42:48.850 align:middle line:84%
One of you deliberately fell
for the trap that I was setting.

00:42:48.850 --> 00:42:51.340 align:middle line:84%
An example where you
convince yourself

00:42:51.340 --> 00:42:55.810 align:middle line:84%
that these two, the Mandarin
and the Armenian words,

00:42:55.810 --> 00:42:58.930 align:middle line:84%
are not related, despite having
some stuff that looks like it's

00:42:58.930 --> 00:42:59.830 align:middle line:90%
in common.

00:42:59.830 --> 00:43:04.340 align:middle line:84%
It's really the Armenian and the
Greek words that are related.

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:07.250 align:middle line:84%
Another example where you have
two words that look similar,

00:43:07.250 --> 00:43:09.460 align:middle line:84%
but the two languages
are not related.

00:43:09.460 --> 00:43:17.410 align:middle line:84%
There's a beautiful memoir by a
guy named Dixon, Robert Dixon.

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:20.180 align:middle line:90%
He's a linguist who did--

00:43:20.180 --> 00:43:23.680 align:middle line:84%
he wrote a book called
Memoirs of a Field Worker

00:43:23.680 --> 00:43:26.620 align:middle line:84%
which was about his work
in Aboriginal languages

00:43:26.620 --> 00:43:29.560 align:middle line:90%
in Australia in the '60s.

00:43:29.560 --> 00:43:31.150 align:middle line:90%
It's a nice book.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:32.650 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
things he talks about

00:43:32.650 --> 00:43:37.060 align:middle line:84%
is going-- he was traveling
around different Aboriginal

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:39.130 align:middle line:84%
areas, talking to
different speakers

00:43:39.130 --> 00:43:40.180 align:middle line:90%
of different languages.

00:43:40.180 --> 00:43:42.130 align:middle line:84%
And there was a
particular language

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:45.205 align:middle line:84%
that he managed to find possibly
one of the last speakers of,

00:43:45.205 --> 00:43:48.100 align:middle line:84%
a language called
Mbabaram, which no longer

00:43:48.100 --> 00:43:49.150 align:middle line:90%
has any speakers at all.

00:43:49.150 --> 00:43:50.710 align:middle line:90%
This was in the '60s.

00:43:50.710 --> 00:43:52.660 align:middle line:84%
And he was very interested
in this language.

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:54.100 align:middle line:84%
It was supposed to be
quite different from some

00:43:54.100 --> 00:43:55.558 align:middle line:84%
of the languages
that were near it.

00:43:55.558 --> 00:43:57.070 align:middle line:84%
And he finally
found a speaker who

00:43:57.070 --> 00:43:59.170 align:middle line:84%
was finally willing to talk to
him and sat down with this guy.

00:43:59.170 --> 00:44:00.790 align:middle line:84%
And the guy said, OK,
let me start teaching

00:44:00.790 --> 00:44:01.960 align:middle line:90%
you some Mbabaram words.

00:44:01.960 --> 00:44:03.930 align:middle line:90%
Do you know our word for "dog"?

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:04.930 align:middle line:90%
And he was like, OK, no.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:06.100 align:middle line:90%
What's your word for "dog"?

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:09.820 align:middle line:84%
And the guy smiled,
and he said, "dog."

00:44:09.820 --> 00:44:13.370 align:middle line:84%
And Dixon was like, OK,
this has to be a joke.

00:44:13.370 --> 00:44:15.980 align:middle line:84%
But it turned out
it was not a joke.

00:44:15.980 --> 00:44:18.340 align:middle line:84%
Most of the languages
of the world

00:44:18.340 --> 00:44:19.870 align:middle line:90%
probably have a word for "dog."

00:44:19.870 --> 00:44:21.880 align:middle line:84%
There are dogs in
lots of places.

00:44:21.880 --> 00:44:25.990 align:middle line:84%
And "d" and "o" and "g," they're
not such uncommon sounds.

00:44:25.990 --> 00:44:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And 6,000 whatever languages
in the world, and yeah,

00:44:29.920 --> 00:44:32.090 align:middle line:84%
this kind of thing
happens every so often.

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:34.400 align:middle line:84%
So these are completely
unrelated languages.

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:38.945 align:middle line:84%
Mbabaram "dog" can
be related to--

00:44:38.945 --> 00:44:41.470 align:middle line:84%
what is that, a proto
[INAUDIBLE] form,

00:44:41.470 --> 00:44:45.670 align:middle line:84%
"gudaga," which changed in
some related languages to other

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:46.690 align:middle line:90%
sounds.

00:44:46.690 --> 00:44:49.750 align:middle line:84%
So you have "gudaga" in Yidiny,
and you have "guda" in Dyirbal.

00:44:49.750 --> 00:44:52.162 align:middle line:84%
And in Mbabaram, they got
rid of the first syllable,

00:44:52.162 --> 00:44:53.620 align:middle line:84%
and they got rid
of the last vowel.

00:44:53.620 --> 00:44:55.420 align:middle line:84%
And before you knew
it, you had "dog."

00:44:55.420 --> 00:44:57.670 align:middle line:90%
English also has a word "dog."

00:44:57.670 --> 00:45:00.382 align:middle line:90%
It's not from Australia.

00:45:00.382 --> 00:45:01.840 align:middle line:84%
So we have-- it
originally referred

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:06.130 align:middle line:84%
to another kind, a particular
kind of dog, a mastiff.

00:45:06.130 --> 00:45:08.320 align:middle line:84%
And it's taken over
as our word for "dog."

00:45:08.320 --> 00:45:12.508 align:middle line:84%
Our older word for "dog" was
"hound," which we still have.

00:45:12.508 --> 00:45:14.300 align:middle line:84%
And this is the kind
of thing that happens.

00:45:14.300 --> 00:45:17.350 align:middle line:84%
So you know, Persian and English
have the same word for "bad."

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:19.840 align:middle line:84%
Malay and Greek have very
similar-looking words

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:21.310 align:middle line:90%
for "eye."

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:23.740 align:middle line:90%
And it's just a coincidence.

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:26.350 align:middle line:84%
How do you know whether you're
looking at a coincidence?

00:45:26.350 --> 00:45:28.900 align:middle line:84%
Well, you ask yourself whether
you can posit regular sound

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:30.010 align:middle line:90%
changes.

00:45:30.010 --> 00:45:33.160 align:middle line:84%
So English and Kaqchikel
Mayan have words for "mess"

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.710 align:middle line:84%
that sound more
or less the same.

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:37.990 align:middle line:84%
That's the kind of
thing, if I just tell you

00:45:37.990 --> 00:45:39.700 align:middle line:84%
in isolation I have
two languages that

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:42.130 align:middle line:84%
have the same word for
"mess," if you want to know,

00:45:42.130 --> 00:45:44.560 align:middle line:84%
are those languages related,
well the way you find out

00:45:44.560 --> 00:45:46.210 align:middle line:84%
is by trying to
find out whether you

00:45:46.210 --> 00:45:48.310 align:middle line:84%
can come up with
sound changes that

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:50.160 align:middle line:90%
apply to more than one word.

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:52.660 align:middle line:84%
Is it generally the case that
where this language has an "m"

00:45:52.660 --> 00:45:53.952 align:middle line:90%
this other language has an "m'?

00:45:53.952 --> 00:45:57.370 align:middle line:84%
Where this language has an "s,"
this other language has an "s"?

00:45:57.370 --> 00:46:00.050 align:middle line:84%
In the case of English and
Kaqchikel, the answer is no.

00:46:00.050 --> 00:46:02.470 align:middle line:84%
So here are a bunch of other
words with "m"s in them,

00:46:02.470 --> 00:46:05.170 align:middle line:84%
and they don't have
"m"s in Kaqchikel.

00:46:05.170 --> 00:46:07.900 align:middle line:84%
You can't rely on
it being the case

00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:10.030 align:middle line:84%
that every English
word with an "m" in it

00:46:10.030 --> 00:46:12.610 align:middle line:84%
would have an "m"
in Kaqchikel, even

00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:14.150 align:middle line:90%
if the languages were related.

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:16.330 align:middle line:84%
But you are entitled
to hope that it'll

00:46:16.330 --> 00:46:18.460 align:middle line:90%
happen more than once.

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:20.830 align:middle line:84%
So this looks like
a coincidence.

00:46:20.830 --> 00:46:23.920 align:middle line:90%


00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:27.160 align:middle line:90%
Any questions about any of this?

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:29.470 align:middle line:84%
Just as an in-class
exercise, I wanted

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.570 align:middle line:84%
to try doing some
historical linguistics,

00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:33.880 align:middle line:84%
reconstructing
some sound changes.

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:35.810 align:middle line:84%
Here are some
Polynesian languages.

00:46:35.810 --> 00:46:38.380 align:middle line:84%
So they are languages
from the Polynesian branch

00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:41.890 align:middle line:84%
of the very large
Austronesian language family.

00:46:41.890 --> 00:46:43.990 align:middle line:84%
Hawaiian, which is spoken
in Hawaii, and Maori,

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:46.990 align:middle line:84%
which is from New Zealand, and
Tongan, which is from Tonga,

00:46:46.990 --> 00:46:50.080 align:middle line:90%
and Samoan, which is from Samoa.

00:46:50.080 --> 00:46:53.500 align:middle line:84%
These are all languages spoken
in islands of the Pacific,

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:54.895 align:middle line:84%
particularly the
eastern Pacific,

00:46:54.895 --> 00:46:57.280 align:middle line:90%
or not too far from Hawaii--

00:46:57.280 --> 00:46:59.680 align:middle line:90%
between Hawaii and New Zealand.

00:46:59.680 --> 00:47:02.290 align:middle line:84%
And these are all words
that look pretty related

00:47:02.290 --> 00:47:03.200 align:middle line:90%
to each other.

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:04.810 align:middle line:84%
And so let's see if
we can figure out

00:47:04.810 --> 00:47:06.400 align:middle line:90%
any sound correspondences.

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:09.680 align:middle line:90%


00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.680 align:middle line:84%
Maybe we can start by
looking at the things

00:47:12.680 --> 00:47:13.925 align:middle line:90%
that I have made green here.

00:47:13.925 --> 00:47:16.460 align:middle line:90%


00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:17.390 align:middle line:90%
What do you think?

00:47:17.390 --> 00:47:20.090 align:middle line:84%
What sort of proto
sound should we

00:47:20.090 --> 00:47:24.015 align:middle line:84%
posit for the sound that
is in green in these kinds

00:47:24.015 --> 00:47:24.515 align:middle line:90%
of examples?

00:47:24.515 --> 00:47:28.897 align:middle line:90%


00:47:28.897 --> 00:47:31.480 align:middle line:84%
There are at least two theories
that we could take seriously--

00:47:31.480 --> 00:47:33.340 align:middle line:84%
hypotheses we could
take seriously, right?

00:47:33.340 --> 00:47:35.830 align:middle line:84%
There's a third
kind of hypothesis.

00:47:35.830 --> 00:47:39.280 align:middle line:90%
Maybe it's an "m," right?

00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:41.990 align:middle line:84%
But let's put that possibility
aside for a second.

00:47:41.990 --> 00:47:44.270 align:middle line:84%
We're not going to do that
unless we're driven to it.

00:47:44.270 --> 00:47:46.270 align:middle line:84%
So the two theories that
we could take seriously

00:47:46.270 --> 00:47:51.110 align:middle line:84%
are maybe it's a "k"
or maybe it's a "t."

00:47:51.110 --> 00:47:54.920 align:middle line:84%
Anybody have a theory
about what we ought to do?

00:47:54.920 --> 00:47:56.390 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:47:56.390 --> 00:48:03.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] --that
maybe the majority rules

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:03.730 align:middle line:90%
and it's the "t."

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:06.063 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, so we
could do this democratically

00:48:06.063 --> 00:48:09.400 align:middle line:84%
and say, yeah, there are more
"t"s on this slide than there

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:11.140 align:middle line:90%
are "k"s.

00:48:11.140 --> 00:48:12.550 align:middle line:90%
Majority rules.

00:48:12.550 --> 00:48:13.425 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:48:13.425 --> 00:48:14.880 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I would
also go with "t"

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:20.300 align:middle line:84%
because Hawaii is a more
geographically isolated area.

00:48:20.300 --> 00:48:21.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:48:21.290 --> 00:48:24.260 align:middle line:84%
All of these-- I mean,
these are all islands.

00:48:24.260 --> 00:48:25.550 align:middle line:90%
Amazing story.

00:48:25.550 --> 00:48:29.060 align:middle line:84%
These people started--
the Proto-Austronesians

00:48:29.060 --> 00:48:30.710 align:middle line:84%
are supposed to have
started in Taiwan

00:48:30.710 --> 00:48:34.280 align:middle line:84%
and headed out in
outrigger canoes

00:48:34.280 --> 00:48:37.250 align:middle line:84%
and settled all of these
islands in the Pacific,

00:48:37.250 --> 00:48:40.100 align:middle line:84%
tossing consonants overboard
when they got to too heavy,

00:48:40.100 --> 00:48:42.362 align:middle line:90%
as far as I can tell.

00:48:42.362 --> 00:48:43.070 align:middle line:90%
But you're right.

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:45.860 align:middle line:84%
Hawaiian was near the
end of their journey.

00:48:45.860 --> 00:48:48.800 align:middle line:84%
So they're supposed to
have started in Taiwan.

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:51.470 align:middle line:84%
New Zealand and
Tonga and Samoa were

00:48:51.470 --> 00:48:54.210 align:middle line:84%
all closer to where they
started than Hawaii.

00:48:54.210 --> 00:48:57.590 align:middle line:84%
So maybe Hawaii-- we're
justified to think

00:48:57.590 --> 00:48:59.750 align:middle line:84%
that Hawaiian might
have undergone some

00:48:59.750 --> 00:49:03.170 align:middle line:90%
of the special sound changes.

00:49:03.170 --> 00:49:05.720 align:middle line:84%
I can also tell you something
special about Hawaiian.

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:08.660 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
this will come up later.

00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:10.580 align:middle line:90%
Hawaiian does not have a "t."

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:14.420 align:middle line:84%
It's one of the few languages
in the world that does not,

00:49:14.420 --> 00:49:17.060 align:middle line:84%
which is why, if
you've ever heard,

00:49:17.060 --> 00:49:21.740 align:middle line:84%
there's a song that is sometimes
played around Christmas

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:25.550 align:middle line:84%
which involves the Hawaiian
version of "Merry Christmas"--

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:29.920 align:middle line:90%
"Mele Kalikimaka."

00:49:29.920 --> 00:49:33.890 align:middle line:84%
"Mele Kalikimaka" is
the Hawaiian version

00:49:33.890 --> 00:49:36.440 align:middle line:84%
of "Merry Christmas"
because Hawaiian has no t,"

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:39.830 align:middle line:84%
and it also has no
"s," So they need

00:49:39.830 --> 00:49:42.260 align:middle line:84%
to replace the "s" in
"Christmas" with something,

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:44.720 align:middle line:84%
and what they replace it
with is "k" because that's

00:49:44.720 --> 00:49:45.660 align:middle line:90%
the best they can do.

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:48.980 align:middle line:84%
That's the closest
they've got to that.

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:50.570 align:middle line:90%
So yes, Hawaiian has no "t."

00:49:50.570 --> 00:49:52.820 align:middle line:84%
So the idea that
maybe there was a t,"

00:49:52.820 --> 00:49:55.400 align:middle line:84%
and in Hawaiian,
it became a "k,"

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:57.860 align:middle line:90%
has some initial plausibility.

00:49:57.860 --> 00:49:59.930 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, we'll posit a
sound change in Hawaiian

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:02.400 align:middle line:90%
from "t" to "k."

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:05.850 align:middle line:84%
And we'll posit a some
Proto-Polynesian forms

00:50:05.850 --> 00:50:07.260 align:middle line:90%
that will have "t" in them.

00:50:07.260 --> 00:50:09.690 align:middle line:84%
That is, they'll look like
the Maori, Tongan, and Samoan

00:50:09.690 --> 00:50:12.180 align:middle line:84%
forms, and then we'll
posit the sound change

00:50:12.180 --> 00:50:16.330 align:middle line:84%
in Hawaiian that changes
that "t" into a "k."

00:50:16.330 --> 00:50:17.980 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:50:17.980 --> 00:50:21.620 align:middle line:90%
Here are some more forms.

00:50:21.620 --> 00:50:22.910 align:middle line:90%
What should we do with these?

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:28.982 align:middle line:90%


00:50:28.982 --> 00:50:30.815 align:middle line:84%
So it's either a "k"
or it's a glottal stop.

00:50:30.815 --> 00:50:40.630 align:middle line:90%


00:50:40.630 --> 00:50:46.000 align:middle line:84%
Maybe for now we should just sit
on this problem because here,

00:50:46.000 --> 00:50:48.070 align:middle line:84%
just this principle of
doing this kind of thing

00:50:48.070 --> 00:50:50.350 align:middle line:84%
democratically, maybe
it doesn't help us.

00:50:50.350 --> 00:50:53.975 align:middle line:84%
We've got two votes for "k"
and two votes for glottal stop.

00:50:53.975 --> 00:50:55.600 align:middle line:84%
This might be a place
where it's easier

00:50:55.600 --> 00:50:58.690 align:middle line:84%
to imagine a "k"
becoming a glottal stop

00:50:58.690 --> 00:51:02.200 align:middle line:84%
than it is to imagine a
glottal stop becoming a "k."

00:51:02.200 --> 00:51:05.110 align:middle line:84%
"k"s becoming glottal stops,
that's a thing that happens

00:51:05.110 --> 00:51:08.980 align:middle line:84%
pretty commonly in
various languages.

00:51:08.980 --> 00:51:12.470 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, that's what people
standardly do in this case.

00:51:12.470 --> 00:51:17.860 align:middle line:84%
So in Hawaiian, we posit a
"k" changing to a global stop

00:51:17.860 --> 00:51:20.560 align:middle line:90%
in these kinds of examples.

00:51:20.560 --> 00:51:22.630 align:middle line:84%
Maori and Tongan
are the ones that

00:51:22.630 --> 00:51:23.890 align:middle line:90%
are being conservative here.

00:51:23.890 --> 00:51:26.480 align:middle line:84%
We'll see an additional reason
to say that in just a second,

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:26.980 align:middle line:90%
I think.

00:51:26.980 --> 00:51:31.520 align:middle line:90%


00:51:31.520 --> 00:51:33.890 align:middle line:84%
Here's the additional
reason to say that.

00:51:33.890 --> 00:51:35.960 align:middle line:84%
Here are a bunch
more words where

00:51:35.960 --> 00:51:41.660 align:middle line:84%
Tongan has glottal stops
in unpredictable places.

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:43.445 align:middle line:84%
So at the beginning
of the word for "day"

00:51:43.445 --> 00:51:46.070 align:middle line:84%
and twice in the word for "love"
and in the middle of the words

00:51:46.070 --> 00:51:49.025 align:middle line:90%
for "leg" and "voice."

00:51:49.025 --> 00:51:51.650 align:middle line:84%
And it's not because-- so if you
want to learn to speak Tongan,

00:51:51.650 --> 00:51:53.108 align:middle line:84%
one of the things
you have to learn

00:51:53.108 --> 00:51:55.610 align:middle line:84%
is that some words begin
with glottal stops,

00:51:55.610 --> 00:51:57.110 align:middle line:90%
and others begin with vowels.

00:51:57.110 --> 00:52:00.320 align:middle line:84%
So "dawn" starts with a
vowel, and "day" starts

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:01.650 align:middle line:90%
with a glottal stop.

00:52:01.650 --> 00:52:05.300 align:middle line:84%
And distinguishing these
from each other is tricky.

00:52:05.300 --> 00:52:08.150 align:middle line:84%
The fact that Tongan is putting
these glottal stops in places

00:52:08.150 --> 00:52:09.560 align:middle line:90%
that can't be predicted--

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:10.100 align:middle line:90%
[SNEEZE]

00:52:10.100 --> 00:52:10.880 align:middle line:90%
Bless you.

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:13.700 align:middle line:90%


00:52:13.700 --> 00:52:16.280 align:middle line:84%
Leads us to think
that what's going on

00:52:16.280 --> 00:52:20.120 align:middle line:84%
is that those were glottal
stops in Proto-Polynesian,

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:22.920 align:middle line:84%
and they've been lost in
all the other languages.

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.480 align:middle line:84%
So here's place
where predictability

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:29.000 align:middle line:90%
trumps democracy.

00:52:29.000 --> 00:52:32.960 align:middle line:84%
We can't predict where Tongan
will have these glottal stops.

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:36.680 align:middle line:84%
We can predict where
Hawaiian, Maori, and Samoan

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:37.880 align:middle line:90%
will have glottal stops.

00:52:37.880 --> 00:52:41.510 align:middle line:84%
So Hawaiian and Samoan
will have glottal stops

00:52:41.510 --> 00:52:44.810 align:middle line:84%
wherever there's a "k"
in Maori and Tongan.

00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:48.530 align:middle line:84%
And Maori just won't
have glottal stops.

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:50.960 align:middle line:84%
So it's always predictable
where the glottal stops

00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:52.100 align:middle line:90%
are in the other languages.

00:52:52.100 --> 00:52:54.017 align:middle line:84%
It's not predictable
where they are in Tongan,

00:52:54.017 --> 00:52:56.280 align:middle line:84%
so we'll posit them
for the proto language.

00:52:56.280 --> 00:53:00.170 align:middle line:84%
So Aloha in Hawaiian, which
literally means "love,"

00:53:00.170 --> 00:53:05.600 align:middle line:84%
in Proto-Polynesian is
"'alo'ofa" with two glottal

00:53:05.600 --> 00:53:06.830 align:middle line:90%
stops in it.

00:53:06.830 --> 00:53:09.200 align:middle line:84%
And you can see, incidentally,
another sound change.

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:14.980 align:middle line:84%
The "f" became "h" in
Hawaiian and also in Maori.

00:53:14.980 --> 00:53:17.230 align:middle line:84%
So Hawaiian has
undergone sound changes,

00:53:17.230 --> 00:53:19.630 align:middle line:84%
"k" becoming glottal
stops, "t" becoming "k,"

00:53:19.630 --> 00:53:22.240 align:middle line:90%
and glottal stop vanishing.

00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:25.000 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes-- so I'll
just do that again dramatically

00:53:25.000 --> 00:53:28.340 align:middle line:90%
with a sagittal section here.

00:53:28.340 --> 00:53:32.410 align:middle line:84%
So Proto-Polynesian "ata,"
"dawn," has become "aka."

00:53:32.410 --> 00:53:35.080 align:middle line:84%
In Hawaiian, "t" has become
"k," with the consequence

00:53:35.080 --> 00:53:37.480 align:middle line:90%
that Hawaiian has no "t."

00:53:37.480 --> 00:53:40.840 align:middle line:84%
"k" has become glottal stop,
so Proto-Polynesian "kula,"

00:53:40.840 --> 00:53:44.320 align:middle line:84%
which means "red," has
become "'ula" in Hawaiian.

00:53:44.320 --> 00:53:47.920 align:middle line:84%
And Proto-Polynesian
glottal stop has vanished.

00:53:47.920 --> 00:53:51.070 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes
called a chain shift.

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:55.300 align:middle line:84%
And there are different ways
of thinking about chain shifts,

00:53:55.300 --> 00:53:56.890 align:middle line:84%
but they're not--
cross-linguistically

00:53:56.890 --> 00:53:57.490 align:middle line:90%
not uncommon.

00:53:57.490 --> 00:53:59.380 align:middle line:84%
I'll show you another
kind of example of one

00:53:59.380 --> 00:54:03.730 align:middle line:84%
in just a second, where you
have sound A becoming sound B,

00:54:03.730 --> 00:54:08.050 align:middle line:84%
sound B becoming sound C,
and sound C becoming sound D.

00:54:08.050 --> 00:54:13.420 align:middle line:84%
And it's often difficult to
determine the order in which

00:54:13.420 --> 00:54:14.770 align:middle line:90%
things like this happen.

00:54:14.770 --> 00:54:17.980 align:middle line:84%
One way to think about this
is the Hawaiians got rid

00:54:17.980 --> 00:54:19.810 align:middle line:84%
of Proto-Polynesian
glottal stop,

00:54:19.810 --> 00:54:22.810 align:middle line:84%
and then they were like, now
that we have no glottal stop,

00:54:22.810 --> 00:54:26.137 align:middle line:84%
we're free to pronounce our
"k" as glottally as we like.

00:54:26.137 --> 00:54:27.970 align:middle line:84%
We don't have to worry
about the "k" getting

00:54:27.970 --> 00:54:29.710 align:middle line:90%
confused with the glottal stop.

00:54:29.710 --> 00:54:31.630 align:middle line:84%
And so the "k" began
drifting backwards

00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:32.920 align:middle line:90%
and becoming a glottal stop.

00:54:32.920 --> 00:54:34.450 align:middle line:84%
And then they were
like, wait, wait.

00:54:34.450 --> 00:54:35.720 align:middle line:90%
We have no "k" anymore.

00:54:35.720 --> 00:54:36.460 align:middle line:90%
Our "k" is gone.

00:54:36.460 --> 00:54:37.450 align:middle line:90%
It's just become a glottal stop.

00:54:37.450 --> 00:54:38.050 align:middle line:90%
We miss "k."

00:54:38.050 --> 00:54:40.510 align:middle line:84%
We will change our
"t" into a "k."

00:54:40.510 --> 00:54:42.400 align:middle line:84%
And then who knows
what would happen,

00:54:42.400 --> 00:54:44.260 align:middle line:84%
how they would manage
to come up with a "t"?

00:54:44.260 --> 00:54:45.760 align:middle line:84%
Maybe if we keep
an eye on Hawaiian,

00:54:45.760 --> 00:54:47.830 align:middle line:90%
we'll get to see what they do.

00:54:47.830 --> 00:54:50.440 align:middle line:84%
It's at least one way to talk
about these kinds of chain

00:54:50.440 --> 00:54:52.995 align:middle line:90%
shifts.

00:54:52.995 --> 00:54:54.370 align:middle line:84%
Another famous
chain shift, which

00:54:54.370 --> 00:54:56.260 align:middle line:84%
is called the Great
English Vowel Shift--

00:54:56.260 --> 00:54:58.930 align:middle line:84%
sorry, the English
Great Vowel Shift.

00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:05.230 align:middle line:84%
It is the reason that if you
study almost any other language

00:55:05.230 --> 00:55:07.960 align:middle line:84%
of Europe, you'll
learn vowels that

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:11.680 align:middle line:84%
have a certain set of
sounds associated with them.

00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:13.300 align:middle line:84%
They are the sounds,
more or less,

00:55:13.300 --> 00:55:15.550 align:middle line:84%
that those symbols
have in the IPA.

00:55:15.550 --> 00:55:17.770 align:middle line:84%
So in lots of
languages, this symbol

00:55:17.770 --> 00:55:20.710 align:middle line:84%
is used for a vowel
that's pronounced "ee."

00:55:20.710 --> 00:55:25.360 align:middle line:84%
But in English,
it's pronounced "I."

00:55:25.360 --> 00:55:29.980 align:middle line:84%
Or that in lots of languages,
this symbol is used for a vowel

00:55:29.980 --> 00:55:33.940 align:middle line:84%
that's pronounced "a," but in
English, it's pronounced "ee."

00:55:33.940 --> 00:55:36.365 align:middle line:84%
This is all because of the
Great English Vowel Shift.

00:55:36.365 --> 00:55:37.990 align:middle line:84%
What was the Great
English Vowel Shift?

00:55:37.990 --> 00:55:39.220 align:middle line:90%
It went like this.

00:55:39.220 --> 00:55:41.860 align:middle line:84%
Around the 14th
century, English had

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:43.660 align:middle line:90%
long vowels in these positions.

00:55:43.660 --> 00:55:45.310 align:middle line:90%
English also had short vowels.

00:55:45.310 --> 00:55:46.420 align:middle line:90%
We'll put those aside.

00:55:46.420 --> 00:55:48.420 align:middle line:84%
So there were long vowels,
that were just vowels

00:55:48.420 --> 00:55:50.440 align:middle line:90%
that were held for a long time.

00:55:50.440 --> 00:55:54.280 align:middle line:84%
And they were in those
positions in the mouth.

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:56.980 align:middle line:84%
So you had an "ee,"
an "ay," an "aa,"

00:55:56.980 --> 00:55:59.590 align:middle line:84%
and then an "ah,"
"aw," "oh," and "oo."

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:01.780 align:middle line:90%
We had all those long vowels.

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:09.710 align:middle line:84%
And then the "ee" and the
"oo" became dipthongs.

00:56:09.710 --> 00:56:13.160 align:middle line:84%
If you think about holding
an "ee" for a long time,

00:56:13.160 --> 00:56:16.870 align:middle line:84%
your tongue is in a
position of maximum tension.

00:56:16.870 --> 00:56:19.480 align:middle line:84%
It's front and high,
and you're supposed

00:56:19.480 --> 00:56:20.893 align:middle line:90%
to hold it for a long time.

00:56:20.893 --> 00:56:22.810 align:middle line:84%
And you can understand
how something like that

00:56:22.810 --> 00:56:24.268 align:middle line:84%
might have changed
into a dipthong.

00:56:24.268 --> 00:56:26.800 align:middle line:84%
So instead of holding
an "ee" for a long time,

00:56:26.800 --> 00:56:29.350 align:middle line:84%
you were allowing
yourself to begin

00:56:29.350 --> 00:56:31.300 align:middle line:84%
the vowel with your
tongue in some more

00:56:31.300 --> 00:56:33.490 align:middle line:84%
central position and
then just sort of gesture

00:56:33.490 --> 00:56:34.690 align:middle line:90%
in the direction of an "ee."

00:56:34.690 --> 00:56:39.520 align:middle line:84%
So you went from "ee" to
"uee" to "ayee" to "ai."

00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:44.710 align:middle line:84%
And so "ee" and "oo"
became "ai" and "ow."

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:46.930 align:middle line:84%
And then suddenly,
English was like, wait.

00:56:46.930 --> 00:56:48.520 align:middle line:84%
Didn't we have an
"ee" and an "oo"?

00:56:48.520 --> 00:56:49.810 align:middle line:90%
Our "ee" and "oo" are gone.

00:56:49.810 --> 00:56:52.030 align:middle line:90%
All we have is "ai" and "ow."

00:56:52.030 --> 00:56:55.250 align:middle line:90%
And so "ay" and "oh" changed.

00:56:55.250 --> 00:56:59.410 align:middle line:84%
So "ay" became "ee"
and "oh" became "oo,"

00:56:59.410 --> 00:57:05.980 align:middle line:84%
filling the gap that the first
part of the vowel shift left.

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:09.370 align:middle line:84%
And then long "aa" and
"ah" both became "ay."

00:57:09.370 --> 00:57:16.420 align:middle line:84%
That's why long "a" in a word
like "fate" is pronounced "ay."

00:57:16.420 --> 00:57:22.070 align:middle line:84%
It's because, well, the "ay"
was off becoming an "ee,"

00:57:22.070 --> 00:57:24.340 align:middle line:84%
and so now we had a space
where there was an "ay."

00:57:24.340 --> 00:57:26.395 align:middle line:90%
And so our "ah" became an "ay."

00:57:26.395 --> 00:57:29.260 align:middle line:90%
And our "aw" became an "oh."

00:57:29.260 --> 00:57:33.790 align:middle line:84%
So all of our vowels have
undergone this dance.

00:57:33.790 --> 00:57:38.440 align:middle line:84%
And if you look at
modern English dialects,

00:57:38.440 --> 00:57:40.750 align:middle line:84%
you can see a lot of these
kinds of sound changes

00:57:40.750 --> 00:57:41.660 align:middle line:90%
continuing today.

00:57:41.660 --> 00:57:44.500 align:middle line:84%
So I come from a place
where those dipthongs are

00:57:44.500 --> 00:57:51.460 align:middle line:84%
getting flattened, where "ai"
has a tendency to become "aa."

00:57:51.460 --> 00:57:55.870 align:middle line:84%
And so the vowels are continuing
to chase each other around

00:57:55.870 --> 00:57:57.742 align:middle line:90%
in the vowel space.

00:57:57.742 --> 00:57:59.200 align:middle line:84%
I think I talked
about this when we

00:57:59.200 --> 00:58:01.210 align:middle line:84%
were talking about
phonetic inventories,

00:58:01.210 --> 00:58:07.150 align:middle line:84%
that there's work on the
kinds of vowel inventories

00:58:07.150 --> 00:58:08.050 align:middle line:90%
that we find.

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:10.120 align:middle line:84%
So there are plenty of
languages out there that

00:58:10.120 --> 00:58:12.662 align:middle line:84%
have three vowels, and
the three vowels that you

00:58:12.662 --> 00:58:16.330 align:middle line:84%
have if you have three vowels
are "ee," "oo," and "ah."

00:58:16.330 --> 00:58:18.760 align:middle line:84%
That is, there's a
front high vowel,

00:58:18.760 --> 00:58:22.282 align:middle line:84%
there's a back high vowel,
and there's a low vowel.

00:58:22.282 --> 00:58:23.740 align:middle line:84%
That is, you have
three vowels that

00:58:23.740 --> 00:58:26.327 align:middle line:84%
are kind of as far apart
from each other as possible.

00:58:26.327 --> 00:58:28.660 align:middle line:84%
There aren't languages out
there that have three vowels,

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:33.280 align:middle line:84%
and the three vowels are
"ee," "ey," and "eh."

00:58:33.280 --> 00:58:34.877 align:middle line:90%
That's not a vowel system.

00:58:34.877 --> 00:58:36.460 align:middle line:84%
One of my colleagues,
Edward Flemming,

00:58:36.460 --> 00:58:39.220 align:middle line:84%
has done a lot of work on
different kinds of vowel

00:58:39.220 --> 00:58:41.500 align:middle line:84%
systems and reducing
them to this idea

00:58:41.500 --> 00:58:44.470 align:middle line:84%
that you're trying to
keep your vowels maximally

00:58:44.470 --> 00:58:48.710 align:middle line:84%
dispersed from each
other in the vocal tract.

00:58:48.710 --> 00:58:50.710 align:middle line:84%
And that kind of thinking
can be one of the ways

00:58:50.710 --> 00:58:54.070 align:middle line:84%
to think about why English
underwent this vowel shift.

00:58:54.070 --> 00:58:58.630 align:middle line:84%
It's like, first the high
vowels changed into dipthongs,

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:01.108 align:middle line:84%
and then you have this sort
of unclaimed real estate

00:59:01.108 --> 00:59:02.650 align:middle line:84%
at the top of the
vocal tract, right?

00:59:02.650 --> 00:59:04.840 align:middle line:84%
So once you no longer
have an "ee" or an "oo,"

00:59:04.840 --> 00:59:07.330 align:middle line:84%
once they've changed
to "ay" and "ow,"

00:59:07.330 --> 00:59:09.850 align:middle line:84%
there's this desire
for dispersion

00:59:09.850 --> 00:59:14.740 align:middle line:84%
that's pushing you to get
some vowels into that space.

00:59:14.740 --> 00:59:17.155 align:middle line:84%
That first change kind of
triggers a second change.

00:59:17.155 --> 00:59:22.360 align:middle line:90%


00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:25.450 align:middle line:84%
If you study languages,
one of the things

00:59:25.450 --> 00:59:27.520 align:middle line:84%
that you will
sometimes be told is

00:59:27.520 --> 00:59:30.490 align:middle line:84%
here's a regular rule
for how you form, say,

00:59:30.490 --> 00:59:32.440 align:middle line:84%
different forms of
a noun, singulars

00:59:32.440 --> 00:59:34.970 align:middle line:84%
and plurals or different
case forms or whatever.

00:59:34.970 --> 00:59:37.480 align:middle line:84%
And then here are
some irregular forms.

00:59:37.480 --> 00:59:39.820 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes the
irregular forms

00:59:39.820 --> 00:59:42.440 align:middle line:84%
are the consequence
of sound changes.

00:59:42.440 --> 00:59:45.940 align:middle line:84%
So for example,
when I was briefly

00:59:45.940 --> 00:59:48.200 align:middle line:84%
attempting to learn
Latin in high school,

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:52.420 align:middle line:84%
we were taught there
are certain Latin nouns,

00:59:52.420 --> 00:59:55.120 align:middle line:84%
so-called third declension
nouns, for which you

00:59:55.120 --> 00:59:57.910 align:middle line:84%
need to memorize both
the nominative singular

00:59:57.910 --> 01:00:00.700 align:middle line:90%
form and another form.

01:00:00.700 --> 01:00:02.710 align:middle line:84%
We were taught to memorize
the genitive singular

01:00:02.710 --> 01:00:09.010 align:middle line:84%
form because you can't predict
from the nominative singular

01:00:09.010 --> 01:00:10.420 align:middle line:90%
what the other forms will be.

01:00:10.420 --> 01:00:12.640 align:middle line:84%
So you get words like
"rex," which means "king,"

01:00:12.640 --> 01:00:16.720 align:middle line:84%
and "nox," which means "night,"
and "vox," which means "voice."

01:00:16.720 --> 01:00:22.390 align:middle line:84%
But the stems to which
you add any other suffix

01:00:22.390 --> 01:00:24.560 align:middle line:84%
are unpredictable
from the nominatives.

01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:29.050 align:middle line:84%
So king, "rex," the genitive
and the accusative and all

01:00:29.050 --> 01:00:33.280 align:middle line:84%
the other forms are formed off
of a stem "reg-" Or for night,

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:36.730 align:middle line:84%
it's "noct-," and for
a voice, it's "voc-."

01:00:36.730 --> 01:00:38.150 align:middle line:90%
Anybody else here study Latin?

01:00:38.150 --> 01:00:40.233 align:middle line:84%
This is all stuff that you
have to learn if you're

01:00:40.233 --> 01:00:41.350 align:middle line:90%
trying to learn Latin.

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:42.850 align:middle line:84%
And what I was
taught, at least, was

01:00:42.850 --> 01:00:45.580 align:middle line:84%
to think of "king," the word
for king as "rex, regis."

01:00:45.580 --> 01:00:49.220 align:middle line:84%
We were supposed to just
sort of cite both of those.

01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:51.940 align:middle line:84%
So my old high school
Latin teacher, Mrs. McNair,

01:00:51.940 --> 01:00:53.110 align:middle line:90%
that's what she taught us.

01:00:53.110 --> 01:00:55.527 align:middle line:84%
Mrs. McNair could have taught
us-- she probably knew this,

01:00:55.527 --> 01:00:59.830 align:middle line:84%
but she didn't reveal it to us--
that although you can't predict

01:00:59.830 --> 01:01:02.350 align:middle line:84%
the genitive from
the nominative,

01:01:02.350 --> 01:01:04.930 align:middle line:84%
you can predict the
nominative from the genitive.

01:01:04.930 --> 01:01:08.260 align:middle line:84%
So what's really happening in
Latin is that you've got these

01:01:08.260 --> 01:01:11.440 align:middle line:84%
stems, which are
"reg-," "noct-," "voc-,"

01:01:11.440 --> 01:01:14.770 align:middle line:84%
and that you're forming the
nominative by adding an "s"

01:01:14.770 --> 01:01:16.730 align:middle line:84%
and then doing
some sound changes.

01:01:16.730 --> 01:01:19.840 align:middle line:84%
So you take "reg-"
and you add an "s."

01:01:19.840 --> 01:01:21.820 align:middle line:84%
That by itself would
give you "regs."

01:01:21.820 --> 01:01:24.610 align:middle line:84%
And then Latin doesn't
allow a sequence

01:01:24.610 --> 01:01:26.440 align:middle line:90%
of a "g" followed by an "s."

01:01:26.440 --> 01:01:27.840 align:middle line:90%
You make them both voiceless.

01:01:27.840 --> 01:01:29.920 align:middle line:84%
You change the "g"
so that it's "k."

01:01:29.920 --> 01:01:33.640 align:middle line:84%
and Latin spells the
sequence "ks" with an "x."

01:01:33.640 --> 01:01:38.680 align:middle line:84%
Or "nox," yeah, that would
end in two stops followed

01:01:38.680 --> 01:01:39.310 align:middle line:90%
by an "s."

01:01:39.310 --> 01:01:41.140 align:middle line:84%
A "k," a "t"-- a
"k" sound, which

01:01:41.140 --> 01:01:45.022 align:middle line:84%
is spelled in Latin with a
"c," and a "t" and then an "s."

01:01:45.022 --> 01:01:47.260 align:middle line:84%
And then you simplify
that by getting rid

01:01:47.260 --> 01:01:49.720 align:middle line:90%
of the "t" in between.

01:01:49.720 --> 01:01:52.510 align:middle line:84%
Or "vox," which is, well,
basically, just "vocs,"

01:01:52.510 --> 01:01:54.160 align:middle line:90%
just adding "s" to the stem.

01:01:54.160 --> 01:01:57.940 align:middle line:84%
So Mrs. McNair could
have made my life easier

01:01:57.940 --> 01:02:01.095 align:middle line:84%
if she had been willing
to tell me about that.

01:02:01.095 --> 01:02:02.470 align:middle line:84%
So there have to
be sound changes

01:02:02.470 --> 01:02:04.990 align:middle line:84%
that if you're willing to think
about the third declension

01:02:04.990 --> 01:02:06.270 align:middle line:90%
nouns this way--

01:02:06.270 --> 01:02:07.450 align:middle line:90%
[COUGHING] excuse me.

01:02:07.450 --> 01:02:10.870 align:middle line:84%
You can think of them as just
having a nominative singular

01:02:10.870 --> 01:02:14.350 align:middle line:84%
form "s" and some sound
changes that obscure

01:02:14.350 --> 01:02:17.500 align:middle line:84%
the fact that the nominative
singular is really not

01:02:17.500 --> 01:02:20.657 align:middle line:90%
so complicated.

01:02:20.657 --> 01:02:22.990 align:middle line:84%
She would have had to teach
us a bunch of sound changes,

01:02:22.990 --> 01:02:26.110 align:middle line:90%
but these are some of them.

01:02:26.110 --> 01:02:30.130 align:middle line:84%
Or here's another
example of this kind.

01:02:30.130 --> 01:02:31.990 align:middle line:84%
We had a problem set
earlier on Inupiaq,

01:02:31.990 --> 01:02:34.420 align:middle line:84%
which is one of a
number of languages that

01:02:34.420 --> 01:02:35.770 align:middle line:90%
are all related to each other.

01:02:35.770 --> 01:02:37.570 align:middle line:84%
They're all members
of what is still,

01:02:37.570 --> 01:02:40.360 align:middle line:84%
for some reason, called the
Eskimo family of languages,

01:02:40.360 --> 01:02:43.150 align:middle line:84%
where I say "still"
because the word "Eskimo"

01:02:43.150 --> 01:02:47.980 align:middle line:84%
is not well-regarded among
the people to whom it refers.

01:02:47.980 --> 01:02:51.790 align:middle line:84%
It's a name for them that
was come up with by the Cree.

01:02:51.790 --> 01:02:55.330 align:middle line:84%
There are debates
about what it means.

01:02:55.330 --> 01:02:56.930 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't
matter what it means.

01:02:56.930 --> 01:02:58.472 align:middle line:84%
They don't like
being called Eskimos,

01:02:58.472 --> 01:03:01.600 align:middle line:84%
so it's unfortunate that
linguistics continues

01:03:01.600 --> 01:03:03.160 align:middle line:84%
to refer to their
language family

01:03:03.160 --> 01:03:05.260 align:middle line:90%
as the Proto-Eskimo family.

01:03:05.260 --> 01:03:08.710 align:middle line:84%
Here are some words in
this language family.

01:03:08.710 --> 01:03:10.840 align:middle line:84%
These are proto forms,
and so I should really

01:03:10.840 --> 01:03:12.520 align:middle line:90%
have stars on them.

01:03:12.520 --> 01:03:14.800 align:middle line:84%
So "iglu," which is a
word you may have heard,

01:03:14.800 --> 01:03:17.450 align:middle line:90%
it's the word for house.

01:03:17.450 --> 01:03:18.910 align:middle line:90%
So all of us live in igloos.

01:03:18.910 --> 01:03:23.200 align:middle line:84%
We all grew up in igloos, as far
as the language is concerned,

01:03:23.200 --> 01:03:24.640 align:middle line:90%
or "tuma" or "tavsi."

01:03:24.640 --> 01:03:26.350 align:middle line:90%
Here are some words.

01:03:26.350 --> 01:03:29.290 align:middle line:84%
And there's a very
general plural suffix,

01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:33.490 align:middle line:90%
"t," which gets added.

01:03:33.490 --> 01:03:36.670 align:middle line:84%
The proto language for
this language family had--

01:03:36.670 --> 01:03:39.220 align:middle line:90%
let's see now-- four vowels.

01:03:39.220 --> 01:03:42.107 align:middle line:84%
So there was "a," "i," and
"u," and there's also schwa.

01:03:42.107 --> 01:03:43.690 align:middle line:84%
You can see there's
a schwa at the end

01:03:43.690 --> 01:03:46.450 align:middle line:90%
of the word for "footprint."

01:03:46.450 --> 01:03:50.770 align:middle line:84%
And then in the history
between the proto language

01:03:50.770 --> 01:03:55.090 align:middle line:84%
and modern Inupiaq, two
sound changes took place.

01:03:55.090 --> 01:03:58.492 align:middle line:84%
First, "t," like the
"t" of the plural,

01:03:58.492 --> 01:03:59.950 align:middle line:84%
but not just the
"t" of the plural.

01:03:59.950 --> 01:04:01.200 align:middle line:90%
It was a general sound change.

01:04:01.200 --> 01:04:05.770 align:middle line:84%
Whenever you had a "t" that
followed the vowel "i," the "t"

01:04:05.770 --> 01:04:07.403 align:middle line:84%
underwent what's
called palatalization,

01:04:07.403 --> 01:04:09.070 align:middle line:84%
cross-linguistically
a very common sound

01:04:09.070 --> 01:04:14.230 align:middle line:84%
change where a "t" becomes
a "ch" around a front vowel.

01:04:14.230 --> 01:04:16.270 align:middle line:84%
We had something
like this happen

01:04:16.270 --> 01:04:17.470 align:middle line:90%
in English several times.

01:04:17.470 --> 01:04:20.725 align:middle line:84%
It's why our word for cheese
starts with a "ch" sound.

01:04:20.725 --> 01:04:26.690 align:middle line:84%
It comes from Latin caseus,
which started with a "k" sound.

01:04:26.690 --> 01:04:29.320 align:middle line:84%
So T became "ch"
after the vowel "i."

01:04:29.320 --> 01:04:32.290 align:middle line:84%
That's what you're seeing at
the end of the word for "belts."

01:04:32.290 --> 01:04:33.710 align:middle line:90%
Regular sound change.

01:04:33.710 --> 01:04:37.278 align:middle line:84%
And then another
sound change, Inupiaq

01:04:37.278 --> 01:04:38.320 align:middle line:90%
has a three vowel system.

01:04:38.320 --> 01:04:39.760 align:middle line:90%
It only has "a," "i," and "u."

01:04:39.760 --> 01:04:41.140 align:middle line:90%
They got rid of schwa.

01:04:41.140 --> 01:04:44.133 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, they
changed schwa to an "i."

01:04:44.133 --> 01:04:45.550 align:middle line:84%
But these sound
changes took place

01:04:45.550 --> 01:04:47.590 align:middle line:84%
in this order with
the consequence

01:04:47.590 --> 01:04:50.830 align:middle line:84%
that if you want to learn
Inupiaq now, what you're taught

01:04:50.830 --> 01:04:55.330 align:middle line:84%
is, well, the plural suffix is
a "t," except after some nouns,

01:04:55.330 --> 01:04:59.260 align:middle line:84%
but not all nouns, that have
an "i" at the end of them,

01:04:59.260 --> 01:05:00.730 align:middle line:90%
where the "t" becomes a "ch."

01:05:00.730 --> 01:05:04.900 align:middle line:84%
And you just have to learn
which nouns that end in "i"

01:05:04.900 --> 01:05:06.730 align:middle line:84%
change the "t" to
a "ch" and which

01:05:06.730 --> 01:05:08.620 align:middle line:84%
nouns that end in
"i"-- the vowel that's

01:05:08.620 --> 01:05:10.750 align:middle line:90%
written with the letter "i"--

01:05:10.750 --> 01:05:11.410 align:middle line:90%
don't do that.

01:05:11.410 --> 01:05:14.800 align:middle line:84%
So "belt," you just
have to memorize,

01:05:14.800 --> 01:05:16.600 align:middle line:84%
that's the kind of
"i" final noun where

01:05:16.600 --> 01:05:18.610 align:middle line:90%
the plural suffix is a "ch."

01:05:18.610 --> 01:05:22.450 align:middle line:84%
"Footprint," that's the kind
of "i" final where the plural

01:05:22.450 --> 01:05:25.840 align:middle line:90%
suffix is still a "t."

01:05:25.840 --> 01:05:28.930 align:middle line:84%
This situation, which is, of
course, distressing to people

01:05:28.930 --> 01:05:32.263 align:middle line:84%
who want to learn Inupiaq,
is the consequence

01:05:32.263 --> 01:05:34.180 align:middle line:84%
of these sound changes
occurring in this order

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:35.800 align:middle line:84%
and creating this
kind of opacity.

01:05:35.800 --> 01:05:38.200 align:middle line:84%
So what's really going
on is, well, there

01:05:38.200 --> 01:05:39.880 align:middle line:90%
used to be another vowel.

01:05:39.880 --> 01:05:43.600 align:middle line:84%
And we got rid of that
vowel, but we were already

01:05:43.600 --> 01:05:46.250 align:middle line:84%
committed to having "ch" in
certain places and not others.

01:05:46.250 --> 01:05:49.720 align:middle line:84%
I should say there's now dialect
variation within Inupiaq.

01:05:49.720 --> 01:05:52.000 align:middle line:84%
There are dialects of
Inupiaq that said, no, look,

01:05:52.000 --> 01:05:53.110 align:middle line:90%
this is silly.

01:05:53.110 --> 01:05:56.470 align:middle line:84%
We're going to change the
"ch" in "belts" to a "t."

01:05:56.470 --> 01:05:59.320 align:middle line:84%
So they still have "t" becoming
"ch" in some other places,

01:05:59.320 --> 01:06:01.570 align:middle line:84%
but specifically the plural
has gotten regularized

01:06:01.570 --> 01:06:03.260 align:middle line:90%
so that it's a "t" everywhere.

01:06:03.260 --> 01:06:07.130 align:middle line:90%


01:06:07.130 --> 01:06:08.930 align:middle line:84%
Consequence of a
history of sound changes

01:06:08.930 --> 01:06:10.340 align:middle line:90%
is sometimes opacity.

01:06:10.340 --> 01:06:11.990 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of like
the Lardil example

01:06:11.990 --> 01:06:13.250 align:middle line:90%
that I showed you before.

01:06:13.250 --> 01:06:15.830 align:middle line:84%
You have a change
from A to B, and you

01:06:15.830 --> 01:06:19.340 align:middle line:84%
have another change from C
to D. And the consequence

01:06:19.340 --> 01:06:23.690 align:middle line:84%
of the change from C to D would
create new environments for A

01:06:23.690 --> 01:06:25.640 align:middle line:84%
to B, but A to B has
already happened.

01:06:25.640 --> 01:06:28.050 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't happen again.

01:06:28.050 --> 01:06:29.840 align:middle line:84%
So as you are
learning languages,

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:32.030 align:middle line:84%
if you encounter
facts like this,

01:06:32.030 --> 01:06:34.100 align:middle line:84%
entertain the possibility
that what's going on

01:06:34.100 --> 01:06:36.500 align:middle line:84%
is opacity because of
sound change occurring

01:06:36.500 --> 01:06:37.460 align:middle line:90%
in a particular order.

01:06:37.460 --> 01:06:39.140 align:middle line:84%
I don't know whether
you'll consider--

01:06:39.140 --> 01:06:40.910 align:middle line:90%
whether you'll have--

01:06:40.910 --> 01:06:43.370 align:middle line:84%
whether you'll get any
comfort from that thought

01:06:43.370 --> 01:06:46.340 align:middle line:84%
or whether it'll just
annoy you, but that's

01:06:46.340 --> 01:06:49.100 align:middle line:90%
sometimes the consequence.

01:06:49.100 --> 01:06:49.850 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, Lardil.

01:06:49.850 --> 01:06:52.970 align:middle line:90%
We just did Lardil.

01:06:52.970 --> 01:06:53.810 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yeah.

01:06:53.810 --> 01:06:56.300 align:middle line:84%
Here's another example of
the same kind of thing.

01:06:56.300 --> 01:07:00.440 align:middle line:84%
Passamaquoddy is an Algonquian
language spoken up in Maine.

01:07:00.440 --> 01:07:02.870 align:middle line:84%
It underwent a sound
change that deleted

01:07:02.870 --> 01:07:06.663 align:middle line:84%
odd-numbered short
vowels in words,

01:07:06.663 --> 01:07:08.330 align:middle line:84%
depending on the
consonants around them.

01:07:08.330 --> 01:07:11.180 align:middle line:90%
It's called syncope.

01:07:11.180 --> 01:07:14.660 align:middle line:84%
If you started with a word
that meant "I hook a fish,"

01:07:14.660 --> 01:07:17.870 align:middle line:84%
that would have been something
like "nuh-puhteeheek."

01:07:17.870 --> 01:07:22.820 align:middle line:84%
And what you did was to get rid
of the first and third vowels

01:07:22.820 --> 01:07:24.360 align:middle line:90%
in that word.

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:27.502 align:middle line:84%
So in modern Passamaquoddy,
that's now "npuh-teeg."

01:07:27.502 --> 01:07:29.960 align:middle line:84%
So you've gotten rid of the
first vowel and the third vowel

01:07:29.960 --> 01:07:31.250 align:middle line:90%
in the word.

01:07:31.250 --> 01:07:34.490 align:middle line:84%
The "nuh-" at the beginning of
that is a prefix that agrees

01:07:34.490 --> 01:07:36.551 align:middle line:90%
with the subject, "I."

01:07:36.551 --> 01:07:39.225 align:middle line:84%
If you get rid of that prefix,
like if you want to say he

01:07:39.225 --> 01:07:41.600 align:middle line:84%
or she hooks a fish, well then
you don't have that prefix

01:07:41.600 --> 01:07:42.980 align:middle line:90%
and that changes the count.

01:07:42.980 --> 01:07:47.000 align:middle line:84%
So original
"puh-tee-hee-geh," again,

01:07:47.000 --> 01:07:49.470 align:middle line:84%
you've gotten rid of the first
and third vowels in that.

01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:52.490 align:middle line:84%
So in modern Passamaquoddy,
it's "tee-gee-geh."

01:07:52.490 --> 01:07:54.745 align:middle line:84%
So Passamaquoddy
speakers, if they

01:07:54.745 --> 01:07:56.120 align:middle line:84%
want to learn
Passamaquoddy, they

01:07:56.120 --> 01:07:59.270 align:middle line:84%
have to learn if you add
the prefix, you sometimes

01:07:59.270 --> 01:08:01.110 align:middle line:90%
change which vowels are where.

01:08:01.110 --> 01:08:04.430 align:middle line:90%
So "I hook a fish" is "npu-tig."

01:08:04.430 --> 01:08:07.310 align:middle line:84%
But "he or she hooks a
fish" is "tee-hee-guh."

01:08:07.310 --> 01:08:10.490 align:middle line:84%
There's a vowel between the "p"
and the "t" in the "I" form,

01:08:10.490 --> 01:08:11.870 align:middle line:90%
but not in the "he or she" form.

01:08:11.870 --> 01:08:13.880 align:middle line:84%
There is a vowel between
the "t" and the "h"

01:08:13.880 --> 01:08:16.609 align:middle line:84%
in the "he or she" form,
but not in the "I" form.

01:08:16.609 --> 01:08:19.220 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the online
Passamaquoddy dictionary,

01:08:19.220 --> 01:08:21.290 align:middle line:84%
you see lots of examples
of this kind of thing.

01:08:21.290 --> 01:08:24.529 align:middle line:84%
Verbs are listed in
their third-person form,

01:08:24.529 --> 01:08:27.828 align:middle line:84%
but then there will be
a listing for each verb.

01:08:27.828 --> 01:08:29.870 align:middle line:84%
This is what it looks like
when you add a prefix.

01:08:29.870 --> 01:08:32.600 align:middle line:84%
And it's sometimes radically
different as a consequence

01:08:32.600 --> 01:08:34.140 align:middle line:90%
of this sound change.

01:08:34.140 --> 01:08:37.189 align:middle line:84%
So for example, here's another
instance of the same thing.

01:08:37.189 --> 01:08:39.260 align:middle line:84%
"I'm sorry about
it" was originally

01:08:39.260 --> 01:08:42.050 align:middle line:90%
"nuh-muh-zah-kay-in."

01:08:42.050 --> 01:08:46.340 align:middle line:84%
Getting rid of the first and
third vowels in that would have

01:08:46.340 --> 01:08:50.510 align:middle line:84%
given you "nmuh-skey-in,"
which is the modern way to say

01:08:50.510 --> 01:08:51.740 align:middle line:90%
"I'm sorry about it."

01:08:51.740 --> 01:08:55.055 align:middle line:84%
If you don't add the "nuh"
prefix, if you want to say

01:08:55.055 --> 01:08:56.930 align:middle line:84%
"he or she is sorry,"
you're going to get rid

01:08:56.930 --> 01:08:58.550 align:middle line:90%
of the first and third vowels.

01:08:58.550 --> 01:09:02.359 align:middle line:84%
If nothing else happened, that
would give you "sah-key-oo."

01:09:02.359 --> 01:09:07.609 align:middle line:84%
But in fact, "n" next to
"s" tends to become a "p."

01:09:07.609 --> 01:09:11.330 align:middle line:84%
So the word for "I'm sorry
about it" is "nmuh-skey-in,"

01:09:11.330 --> 01:09:14.512 align:middle line:84%
but "he or she is
sorry" is "psah-key-oo"

01:09:14.512 --> 01:09:16.970 align:middle line:84%
with the consequence that these
look so different from each

01:09:16.970 --> 01:09:19.470 align:middle line:84%
other that when I have pointed
out to Passamaquoddy speakers

01:09:19.470 --> 01:09:21.740 align:middle line:84%
that they are the same
word, they're astonished.

01:09:21.740 --> 01:09:23.029 align:middle line:90%
They're like, oh, yeah, right.

01:09:23.029 --> 01:09:25.970 align:middle line:84%
I guess they are, even though
they don't look all that much

01:09:25.970 --> 01:09:28.970 align:middle line:90%
like each other anymore.

01:09:28.970 --> 01:09:32.660 align:middle line:84%
Now, the Passamaquoddy
system looks like the result

01:09:32.660 --> 01:09:33.750 align:middle line:90%
of a stress system.

01:09:33.750 --> 01:09:38.930 align:middle line:84%
So again, the rule is get rid
of the odd-numbered short vowels

01:09:38.930 --> 01:09:40.073 align:middle line:90%
in the sequence.

01:09:40.073 --> 01:09:42.740 align:middle line:84%
And that's the kind of rule that
makes sense if you think, yeah,

01:09:42.740 --> 01:09:45.529 align:middle line:84%
it had a stress
system that stressed

01:09:45.529 --> 01:09:46.970 align:middle line:90%
the even-numbered vowels, right?

01:09:46.970 --> 01:09:51.140 align:middle line:84%
So "I hook a fish" was
"nuh-put-tuh-heek."

01:09:51.140 --> 01:09:53.569 align:middle line:84%
And then they got rid of the
first and the third vowels

01:09:53.569 --> 01:09:54.950 align:middle line:90%
because they weren't stressed.

01:09:54.950 --> 01:09:58.070 align:middle line:84%
And "he or she hooks a
fish" was "puh-tee-hee-geh,"

01:09:58.070 --> 01:10:01.550 align:middle line:84%
and you got rid of the schwa
in the first one because well,

01:10:01.550 --> 01:10:03.170 align:middle line:90%
it wasn't stressed.

01:10:03.170 --> 01:10:04.670 align:middle line:84%
Whether you get rid
of vowels or not

01:10:04.670 --> 01:10:06.830 align:middle line:84%
depends partly on what
consonants are around them.

01:10:06.830 --> 01:10:08.000 align:middle line:84%
That's why you're
not actually getting

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:09.458 align:middle line:84%
rid of the third
vowel in this one.

01:10:09.458 --> 01:10:12.410 align:middle line:90%
You get "tee-hee-geh."

01:10:12.410 --> 01:10:14.600 align:middle line:84%
So if you thought
of Passamaquoddy

01:10:14.600 --> 01:10:18.470 align:middle line:84%
as having stress on its
even-numbered short vowels,

01:10:18.470 --> 01:10:20.210 align:middle line:84%
then you can understand
why it's getting

01:10:20.210 --> 01:10:21.620 align:middle line:84%
rid of its odd-numbered
short vowels.

01:10:21.620 --> 01:10:23.870 align:middle line:84%
It's getting rid of them
because they're not stressed.

01:10:23.870 --> 01:10:25.550 align:middle line:84%
And Passamaquoddy
does have relatives

01:10:25.550 --> 01:10:28.850 align:middle line:84%
that have stress systems
like that, where you stress

01:10:28.850 --> 01:10:31.850 align:middle line:90%
the even-numbered vowels.

01:10:31.850 --> 01:10:34.920 align:middle line:84%
But that is not Passamaquoddy
stress system anymore.

01:10:34.920 --> 01:10:37.877 align:middle line:84%
So Passamaquoddy underwent all
of these sound changes because

01:10:37.877 --> 01:10:39.710 align:middle line:84%
of the stress system
that they used to have,

01:10:39.710 --> 01:10:41.840 align:middle line:84%
that they used to share with
some of their relatives.

01:10:41.840 --> 01:10:43.590 align:middle line:84%
And then they changed
their stress system.

01:10:43.590 --> 01:10:46.310 align:middle line:84%
Their modern stress system
goes "Stress the first vowel

01:10:46.310 --> 01:10:47.960 align:middle line:84%
and stress every
other vowel counting

01:10:47.960 --> 01:10:50.120 align:middle line:90%
backwards from the end."

01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:53.060 align:middle line:84%
So you get words
like [INAUDIBLE],,

01:10:53.060 --> 01:10:54.900 align:middle line:84%
which is "he or she
talks like that,"

01:10:54.900 --> 01:10:56.960 align:middle line:84%
where you're stressing the first
vowel and the second vowel.

01:10:56.960 --> 01:10:59.210 align:middle line:84%
First vowel because you
always stress the first vowel,

01:10:59.210 --> 01:11:00.650 align:middle line:84%
and the second
vowel because it's

01:11:00.650 --> 01:11:03.167 align:middle line:90%
two vowels back from the end.

01:11:03.167 --> 01:11:05.000 align:middle line:84%
So every other vowel,
starting from the end.

01:11:05.000 --> 01:11:06.620 align:middle line:84%
If you make it one
syllable longer,

01:11:06.620 --> 01:11:09.560 align:middle line:84%
then you're going to stress
the first and the third vowel--

01:11:09.560 --> 01:11:14.750 align:middle line:84%
"wee-guh-west-too," "He or she
talks while walking backwards."

01:11:14.750 --> 01:11:17.672 align:middle line:84%
"Quiguh-west-too-bin,"
"You and I like talking."

01:11:17.672 --> 01:11:19.130 align:middle line:84%
So stress is on
the first syllable,

01:11:19.130 --> 01:11:21.770 align:middle line:84%
and it's on the even-numbered
syllables counting backwards

01:11:21.770 --> 01:11:23.250 align:middle line:90%
from the end of the word.

01:11:23.250 --> 01:11:26.030 align:middle line:84%
So they've innovated
this new stress system

01:11:26.030 --> 01:11:28.490 align:middle line:84%
after having this older
stress system which

01:11:28.490 --> 01:11:30.620 align:middle line:84%
created all of
these alternations

01:11:30.620 --> 01:11:31.802 align:middle line:90%
in the positions of vowels.

01:11:31.802 --> 01:11:33.260 align:middle line:84%
There's a reason
that Passamaquoddy

01:11:33.260 --> 01:11:34.617 align:middle line:90%
is difficult to learn.

01:11:34.617 --> 01:11:35.700 align:middle line:90%
There are several reasons.

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:36.290 align:middle line:90%
This is one.

01:11:36.290 --> 01:11:39.883 align:middle line:90%


01:11:39.883 --> 01:11:42.300 align:middle line:84%
So it used to have a different
stress system, during which

01:11:42.300 --> 01:11:43.350 align:middle line:90%
the syncope rule applied.

01:11:43.350 --> 01:11:47.760 align:middle line:84%
And now it has the
system it has now.

01:11:47.760 --> 01:11:49.470 align:middle line:84%
I want to end today
because I think

01:11:49.470 --> 01:11:51.210 align:middle line:84%
we have enough
time for me to heap

01:11:51.210 --> 01:11:54.390 align:middle line:90%
scorn on a couple of things.

01:11:54.390 --> 01:11:56.017 align:middle line:84%
I want to end today
with two bad ideas.

01:11:56.017 --> 01:11:57.600 align:middle line:84%
So one of the reasons
that I'm talking

01:11:57.600 --> 01:11:58.980 align:middle line:84%
to you about historical
linguistics-- there

01:11:58.980 --> 01:11:59.480 align:middle line:90%
are several.

01:11:59.480 --> 01:12:00.775 align:middle line:90%
One is that it's fun.

01:12:00.775 --> 01:12:03.150 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of interesting to
try to trace back these sound

01:12:03.150 --> 01:12:03.720 align:middle line:90%
changes.

01:12:03.720 --> 01:12:05.700 align:middle line:84%
But another is that
historical linguistics

01:12:05.700 --> 01:12:08.430 align:middle line:84%
is the kind of thing that
is sometimes done badly.

01:12:08.430 --> 01:12:11.262 align:middle line:84%
So you'll see in popular
press people saying things

01:12:11.262 --> 01:12:13.470 align:middle line:84%
like, hey, this language is
related to that language,

01:12:13.470 --> 01:12:16.860 align:middle line:84%
or this language has borrowed
this word from that language.

01:12:16.860 --> 01:12:19.710 align:middle line:84%
And if you have any
skepticism about that,

01:12:19.710 --> 01:12:21.210 align:middle line:84%
what you want them
to do is show you

01:12:21.210 --> 01:12:23.640 align:middle line:84%
regular sound correspondences
or regular rules

01:12:23.640 --> 01:12:25.470 align:middle line:84%
for how words are
related to other words.

01:12:25.470 --> 01:12:29.010 align:middle line:84%
So there was a popular
book for a while that

01:12:29.010 --> 01:12:35.940 align:middle line:84%
proposed that the Native
American languages were all

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:40.590 align:middle line:84%
descended from Chinese, that
the Mandarin-- the Chinese

01:12:40.590 --> 01:12:42.990 align:middle line:84%
had encountered
North America first,

01:12:42.990 --> 01:12:46.410 align:middle line:84%
and that their language had
taken over North America,

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:48.000 align:middle line:84%
and that all of the
modern languages

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:49.470 align:middle line:90%
were descended from Chinese.

01:12:49.470 --> 01:12:52.890 align:middle line:84%
And the examples were things
like in modern Mandarin,

01:12:52.890 --> 01:12:56.310 align:middle line:90%
there is a greeting, "nihao."

01:12:56.310 --> 01:12:59.130 align:middle line:84%
And hey, if you watch
Hollywood movies,

01:12:59.130 --> 01:13:02.670 align:middle line:84%
you will hear Native Americans
greeting each other with "how."

01:13:02.670 --> 01:13:05.430 align:middle line:84%
And there, you know,
so clearly these

01:13:05.430 --> 01:13:06.520 align:middle line:90%
are related to each other.

01:13:06.520 --> 01:13:08.700 align:middle line:84%
"How" actually is a
greeting in Lakota.

01:13:08.700 --> 01:13:12.430 align:middle line:84%
It's a greeting that Lakota
men use to greet other men.

01:13:12.430 --> 01:13:15.930 align:middle line:84%
It's not related to the
Mandarin expression "nihao."

01:13:15.930 --> 01:13:18.780 align:middle line:84%
And the reason I can say
that with so much confidence

01:13:18.780 --> 01:13:22.380 align:middle line:84%
is that, well, no one has ever
found sound correspondences

01:13:22.380 --> 01:13:25.080 align:middle line:84%
or anything like that relating
anything else in Lakota

01:13:25.080 --> 01:13:29.010 align:middle line:90%
to anything else in Chinese.

01:13:29.010 --> 01:13:32.490 align:middle line:84%
There are slightly
less silly claims

01:13:32.490 --> 01:13:34.650 align:middle line:84%
that people have made
using what's sometimes

01:13:34.650 --> 01:13:35.950 align:middle line:90%
called megalocomparison.

01:13:35.950 --> 01:13:38.580 align:middle line:84%
So let's skip-- so
glottochronology--

01:13:38.580 --> 01:13:40.710 align:middle line:84%
I'll leave this on the
slide, but glottochronology

01:13:40.710 --> 01:13:43.590 align:middle line:84%
is an attempt to sort of
carbon date language splits.

01:13:43.590 --> 01:13:48.120 align:middle line:84%
So the idea is you take a
list of basic vocabulary.

01:13:48.120 --> 01:13:50.490 align:middle line:84%
You figure out how many
cognates the two languages

01:13:50.490 --> 01:13:54.420 align:middle line:84%
share on the list, and you
start with the assumption

01:13:54.420 --> 01:13:56.940 align:middle line:84%
that cognate loss happens
at a constant rate.

01:13:56.940 --> 01:14:00.000 align:middle line:84%
This is the figure that has been
offered by glottochronologists.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:02.850 align:middle line:90%
And then you do some math.

01:14:02.850 --> 01:14:06.360 align:middle line:84%
And this is something that you
see people seriously saying.

01:14:06.360 --> 01:14:08.400 align:middle line:90%
So if you look in--

01:14:08.400 --> 01:14:10.770 align:middle line:84%
historical linguistics is
usually not done by linguists.

01:14:10.770 --> 01:14:12.960 align:middle line:84%
It's often being
done by biologists.

01:14:12.960 --> 01:14:15.750 align:middle line:84%
They'll say, we know that
these languages are related

01:14:15.750 --> 01:14:17.760 align:middle line:84%
at a time depth of
X. And what they

01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:19.485 align:middle line:84%
mean is we've done
glottochronology,

01:14:19.485 --> 01:14:21.360 align:middle line:84%
which starts from the
assumption that you can

01:14:21.360 --> 01:14:26.040 align:middle line:84%
do this kind of carbon dating
of languages by looking

01:14:26.040 --> 01:14:29.130 align:middle line:84%
at cognate lists and figuring
out the rate at which two

01:14:29.130 --> 01:14:31.780 align:middle line:84%
languages cease to have
cognates with each other.

01:14:31.780 --> 01:14:34.200 align:middle line:84%
The problem is that
the base assumption--

01:14:34.200 --> 01:14:37.560 align:middle line:84%
that cognate loss happens at
a constant rate-- is false.

01:14:37.560 --> 01:14:41.925 align:middle line:84%
And no one has figured
out a way around that.

01:14:41.925 --> 01:14:44.050 align:middle line:84%
There doesn't seem to be
any way to correct for it.

01:14:44.050 --> 01:14:46.830 align:middle line:84%
So if you see people
using glottochronology,

01:14:46.830 --> 01:14:48.660 align:middle line:84%
you should treat
them with skepticism.

01:14:48.660 --> 01:14:53.340 align:middle line:90%


01:14:53.340 --> 01:14:56.130 align:middle line:84%
The other bad idea that's
in here is megalocomparison.

01:14:56.130 --> 01:14:59.640 align:middle line:84%
This is attempts to reconstruct
Proto- World, which were very

01:14:59.640 --> 01:15:01.240 align:middle line:90%
en vogue at a certain time.

01:15:01.240 --> 01:15:03.030 align:middle line:84%
People thought if
we do lots and lots

01:15:03.030 --> 01:15:04.650 align:middle line:84%
of this cross-linguistic
comparison,

01:15:04.650 --> 01:15:07.110 align:middle line:84%
we'll be able to figure out
the ancestor language for all

01:15:07.110 --> 01:15:09.870 align:middle line:90%
of the languages in the world.

01:15:09.870 --> 01:15:14.040 align:middle line:84%
The work has never
involved discovering

01:15:14.040 --> 01:15:17.190 align:middle line:84%
general sound changes that
relate languages to each other.

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:18.937 align:middle line:84%
It always involves
going back to saying,

01:15:18.937 --> 01:15:20.520 align:middle line:84%
"Hey, look, that
word in that language

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:22.437 align:middle line:84%
looks kind of like that
word in that language!

01:15:22.437 --> 01:15:23.430 align:middle line:90%
I bet they're related."

01:15:23.430 --> 01:15:26.070 align:middle line:84%
That's how that
work is always gone.

01:15:26.070 --> 01:15:27.702 align:middle line:84%
So those are two
ideas-- bad ideas

01:15:27.702 --> 01:15:29.160 align:middle line:84%
that you should be
skeptical about.

01:15:29.160 --> 01:15:32.040 align:middle line:84%
This has been my public
service announcement for today.

01:15:32.040 --> 01:15:34.620 align:middle line:84%
Are there any questions about
either of those bad ideas?

01:15:34.620 --> 01:15:38.310 align:middle line:84%
I put more detail about the
bad ideas in the slides.

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:39.240 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

01:15:39.240 --> 01:15:43.430 align:middle line:84%
Go forth, and I will
see you next week.

01:15:43.430 --> 01:15:51.000 align:middle line:90%